verse 1 | part 1 | All praise is for Allah | This opening phrase “All praise is for Allah” (Al-ḥamdu lillāh) is a declaration that every form of praise, gratitude, and perfection belongs exclusively to Allah. It appears at the beginning of Surah Al-Kahf and also opens the entire Quran in Surah Al-Fatihah. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said, “When one of you sneezes, let him say ‘Al-ḥamdu lillāh’ (All praise is due to Allah)” (Sahih Bukhari). This teaches that praise of Allah is due in all circumstances, even in the most trivial events. The phrase encompasses all types of praise: for His attributes, His actions, and His blessings. Historically, the Quraysh pagans also acknowledged Allah as the Creator but associated partners with Him. This verse corrects them, affirming that ultimate praise is due to the One who sent down the Book. The use of the definite article “al” in “al-ḥamd” implies that all comprehensive, pure praise belongs to Allah alone, not shared by idols or false gods. In the context of Surah Al-Kahf, the surah reveals four major stories – the People of the Cave, the owner of two gardens, Musa and Khidr, and Dhul-Qarnayn – all of which demonstrate Allah’s perfect wisdom and power. Thus the praise is directly connected to the revelation that follows. Lesson: a believer’s life starts with gratitude. Recognizing that all good comes from Allah produces humility, contentment, and continuous worship. It also reminds Muslims that the Quran is a magnificent gift, worthy of the highest praise of the Giver.
verse 1 | part 2 | Who has revealed the Book to His servant | The mention of “the Book” refers to the Quran, which was revealed gradually over 23 years to Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), described here as “His servant” (‘abdihi). The word “servant” is one of the most honorable titles, for true greatness lies in complete submission to Allah. The Quran is “revealed” (nazzala), implying a gradual descent from the Preserved Tablet (Al-Lawḥ Al-Maḥfūẓ) to the lowest heaven, and then in portions to the Prophet via Angel Jibreel. This gradual revelation is emphasized in a hadith: “The Quran was sent down in one night to the lowest heaven, then it was revealed to the Messenger of Allah in portions over twenty years” (Mustadrak al-Hakim). Historical context: the Prophet was in Makkah facing intense opposition when this surah was revealed. By calling the Quran “the Book,” Allah affirmed its written preservation even before its compilation, indicating it was destined to be a complete scripture. The Prophet’s role as servant also refutes the Christian claim that Jesus was God’s son, which appears later in verse 4. Islam honors all prophets as servants, not divine beings. Lesson: the Quran is divine guidance, not human invention. Accepting it requires acknowledging the Prophet as Allah’s servant and messenger. The faithful are to follow the Book with the same submission that the Prophet exhibited as a servant.
verse 1 | part 3 | allowing no crookedness in it | The phrase “allowing no crookedness” (wa lam yaj‘al lahu ‘iwajan) means the Quran is free from any distortion, contradiction, error, or deviation from the straight path. The Arabic word ‘iwaj refers to a severe bending or twisting. In a hadith, the Prophet said, “I have left among you something which, if you adhere to it, you will never go astray: the Book of Allah” (Sahih Muslim). This testifies that the Quran’s uprightness is perfect. Historical context: the Quraysh claimed the Quran was poetry, magic, or ancient fables. This verse refutes them by asserting its absolute straightness and consistency. Allah says elsewhere, “Do they not consider the Quran? Had it been from anyone other than Allah, they would have found in it much discrepancy” (4:82). The absence of crookedness also means its laws are just, its stories are true, and its guidance is balanced, neither extreme nor lax. The early Muslims found in this verse confidence when debating with Jews and Christians who believed their scriptures had been altered. Lesson: Truth seekers are urged to approach the Quran with the certainty that it is a flawless guide. When faced with doubts or misunderstandings, the defect lies in human interpretation, not in the divine text. This verse calls believers to trust the Quran completely and to let it be the criterion to straighten their lives.
verse 2 | part 1 | making it perfectly upright | This part continues from verse 1, describing the Quran as “perfectly upright” (qayyiman). While verse 1 negates any crookedness, this verse affirms its absolute straightness and firmness. The word “qayyim” suggests something that stands straight, establishes justice, and maintains order. The Quran is upright in its beliefs, morals, laws, and narratives. It guards and protects the interests of humanity. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The Quran is an intercessor, something given permission to intercede, and it is truthful in its opposition. Whoever places it in front of him, it will lead him to Paradise; whoever puts it behind his back, it will drive him to the Fire” (Sahih Ibn Hibban). Historically, the revelation of this surah came at a time when the Muslim community faced persecution, yet the Quran remained a steady, upright guide, never bending under pressure. Its uprightness also implies that it corrects the distortions introduced into earlier scriptures. For example, the Quran clarifies the true nature of Jesus, which is mentioned later in this surah. Lesson: The Quran’s uprightness demands that Muslims straighten their affairs according to it, not bend the Quran to suit their desires. It is a criterion for justice in all areas of life.
verse 2 | part 2 | to warn the disbelievers of a severe torment from Him | This part explains one purpose of the Quran: to warn those who reject faith of a severe punishment from Allah. The Arabic “ba’san shadīdan” denotes a harsh, painful torment, emphasizing the seriousness of disbelief. The Prophet (peace be upon him) was commanded to be a warner: “O you wrapped in garments, arise and warn” (74:1-2). In a hadith he said, “I am the warner, and death is the overtaker” (Sunan Ibn Majah). Historically, the disbelievers of Makkah mocked warnings of resurrection and punishment. This verse establishes that the threat is real and comes from Allah, not from the Prophet’s own will. The torment could be in this world (like the defeat of the Quraysh at Badr) or the Hereafter (Hellfire). The early Muslims took the warning seriously, reforming their lives. Lesson: Warnings in the Quran are an expression of divine mercy, giving people a chance to repent before it is too late. A believer must maintain a balanced fear of Allah’s punishment along with hope in His mercy.
verse 2 | part 3 | to give good news to the believers—who do good—that they will have a fine reward | Here the Quran also serves to give glad tidings to those who believe and perform righteous deeds (ṣāliḥāt). “Fine reward” translates “ajran ḥasanan,” which is a beautiful, eternal reward: Paradise. Belief (īmān) is not merely intellectual acceptance; it must be coupled with good actions. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Whoever says ‘Lā ilāha illā Allāh’ sincerely will enter Paradise” (Sahih Bukhari). He also stressed that deeds are judged by intentions. Historical context: This gave hope to the oppressed Muslims in Makkah who had left their homes, families, and wealth for the sake of Islam. They were promised an excellent reward that would far surpass their losses. The combination of warning and glad tidings is a common Quranic style, balancing fear and hope so that the believer’s heart is always between the two wings, neither despairing nor complacent. Lesson: good deeds, no matter how small, are not overlooked by Allah. The righteous will find their reward waiting for them, which should motivate consistent action and patience during trials.
verse 3 | part 1 | in which they will remain forever | This part refers to the reward of Paradise, emphasizing its eternal nature. The believers will dwell in it forever, without death, interruption, or fading. The word “khalidīn” indicates eternal abode. In numerous hadith, the Prophet described Paradise as a place of which no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no heart has imagined. He said, “When the people of Paradise enter Paradise, a caller will call out: ‘Indeed, you shall remain healthy and never fall ill; you shall live and never die; you shall be young and never become old; you shall remain happy and never grieve’” (Sahih Muslim). This eternality made the sacrifices of the early Muslims bearable. The Quraysh ridiculed the idea of an eternal afterlife, but this verse firmly asserts it as truth. Lesson: the promise of eternal bliss transforms a believer’s perspective on worldly suffering. It produces patience and steadfastness, knowing that hardships are temporary but the reward is everlasting. It also serves as a deterrent to sin, for hellfire is likewise eternal for those who disbelieve.
verse 4 | part 1 | and to warn those who claim, “Allah has offspring.” | This verse identifies a specific group that needs warning: those who claim Allah has a child. This includes Christians who say Jesus is the son of God, Jews who said Uzayr is the son of God (9:30), and pagan Arabs who considered angels as Allah’s daughters. The Quran categorically rejects such attribution. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Allah says: The son of Adam tells a lie against Me, and he has no right to do so, and he reviles Me, and he has no right to do so. As for his lying about Me, it is his saying, ‘He will not remake me just as He created me’ … As for his reviling Me, it is his saying, ‘Allah has taken a son,’ whereas I am the One, the Everlasting, I beget not nor was I begotten, and there is none comparable to Me’” (Sahih Bukhari). Historically, when this surah was revealed, the Najran Christian delegation had visited the Prophet, leading to a theological debate about Jesus. The surah addresses such beliefs, warning that attributing a son to Allah is a monstrous falsehood. Lesson: Allah’s uniqueness is the foundation of Islamic monotheism. Any association of a child or partner to Him is a direct violation of His majesty, and the one who does so without repentance faces severe punishment.
verse 5 | part 1 | They have no knowledge of this, nor did their forefathers. | This part exposes that the claim about Allah having offspring has no basis in true knowledge. Neither the claimants nor their ancestors had any evidence; it was merely inherited superstition. The Quran frequently criticizes blind following of forefathers without verified knowledge. In a hadith, the Prophet warned against blind imitation: “Do not be blind followers, saying: if people do good, we will do good, and if they do wrong, we will do wrong. But make up your own minds; if people do good, do good, and if they do wrong, do not do wrong” (Sunan At-Tirmidhi). Historical context: the pagan Arabs worshipped idols and believed in the divinity of angels as daughters of Allah, a belief inherited from previous generations without any proof. This verse demolishes that by demanding knowledge, not conjecture. Lesson: Islam is a religion of knowledge, not ignorance. Every belief must be grounded in divine revelation or sound intellect. Blind adherence to tradition is condemned, urging individuals to seek the truth independently.
verse 5 | part 2 | What a terrible claim that comes out of their mouths! They say nothing but lies. | This part expresses the gravity of the false claim. The phrase “kaburat kalimatan” indicates a monstrous, enormously evil word. Their statement is a blatant falsehood (kadhib). In Sahih Bukhari, the Prophet said, “A lie about me is not like a lie about anyone else. Whoever tells a lie about me intentionally, let him take his place in Hellfire.” So, telling a lie about Allah is far graver. Historically, the Quraysh would claim that angels were female and Allah’s daughters, while they themselves preferred sons, exposing a contradictory and fabricated theology. This verse expresses divine outrage, teaching that Muslims must be extremely careful in their speech about Allah. Lesson: Words have severe consequences. Spreading falsehood about Allah is one of the worst sins. Believers must speak only with truth and knowledge, especially in matters of faith, and avoid fabricating or transmitting baseless ideas.
verse 6 | part 1 | Now, perhaps you O Prophet will grieve yourself to death over their denial | This verse addresses the Prophet’s deep sadness over the rejection of his message. The phrase “bakhi‘un nafsaka” means to kill oneself with grief. The Prophet cared immensely for his people’s salvation. Allah consoles him that his grief should not destroy him. The Prophet said, “My example and the example of the people is like a man who started a fire, and when it lighted the surroundings, moths and insects started falling into it. He tried to push them away, but they overpowered him and fell into the fire. This is my example; I am holding you by your waists to keep you away from the Fire, but you are slipping into it” (Sahih Bukhari). Historical context: In Makkah, the Quraysh stubbornly rejected Islam, mocked the Prophet, and tortured his followers. The Prophet was intensely distressed, so this verse comforts him. Lesson: While deep concern for others’ guidance is noble, ultimate guidance is in Allah’s hands. The callers to truth must do their best without excessive personal torment, trusting Allah’s wisdom.
verse 6 | part 2 | if they continue to disbelieve in this message | The “message” (hadha al-ḥadīth) refers to the Quran itself or the discourse of its revelation. The disbelievers’ persistent rejection, even after clear signs, caused the Prophet’s grief. Allah reminds him that their disbelief is a result of their free will, not his failure. In a hadith Qudsi, Allah says, “O My servants, it is only your deeds that I record for you, then I will repay you for them. So whoever finds good, let him praise Allah, and whoever finds otherwise, let him blame no one but himself” (Sahih Muslim). This verse highlights that the Quran leaves no excuse for rejection. The Makkan chiefs admitted the eloquence of the Quran but refused to follow it due to arrogance. Lesson: The truth of the message stands independent of people’s acceptance. Believers must not measure their efforts by results but by obedience to the command. Rejection is a choice of the disbeliever, not a defect in the message.
verse 7 | part 1 | We have indeed made whatever is on earth as an adornment for it | Allah declares that everything on earth – plants, animals, gold, silver, beautiful scenery, and all material attractions – has been made as an adornment (zīnah). This is to test human beings. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The world is sweet and green, and Allah appoints you as successors in it to see how you will behave” (Sahih Muslim). This earthly beauty is not evil in itself; it is a divine design. Historical context: The early Muslims in Madinah were relatively poor compared to the wealthy Quraysh of Makkah. This verse reassures that worldly wealth is a temporary decoration, not a sign of Allah’s pleasure. The adornment of the earth includes power, wealth, families, and technology. Lesson: Earthly adornments are a blessing, but they must not distract from the ultimate purpose. They are a means of test, not an end. The believer engages with them lawfully without being enslaved by them, maintaining the consciousness that they will return to Allah.
verse 7 | part 2 | in order to test which of them is best in deeds | The purpose of the earth’s adornment is iḥtisān and ibtilā’ (testing) to distinguish who performs the best deeds. The emphasis is on quality, not quantity. The Prophet (peace be upon him) explained, “The best of deeds are those that are done consistently, even if they are small” (Sahih Bukhari). Another hadith clarifies that the intention and sincerity behind the deed matter most: “Actions are by intentions” (Sahih Bukhari). Historical context: The Companions understood that poverty or wealth is not an indicator of virtue; a poor person can be best in deeds, just as a wealthy person can be if they use their resources righteously. The test is not to see who is richest but who is most obedient, sincere, and dedicated to Allah. Lesson: Life is an examination hall. The decorations are the test materials; those who are grateful and obedient succeed. One should not envy others’ worldly gains but strive for excellence in worship, charity, and character.
verse 8 | part 1 | And We will certainly reduce whatever is on it to barren ground | This verse reveals the ultimate fate of earth’s adornments: they will be turned into dry, barren soil (ṣa‘īdan juruzan). Allah will remove all vegetation, water, and life, leaving a desolate plain. This is a reference to the end of the world or the destruction that follows death. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The Hour will not be established until the Arabian Peninsula returns to being meadows and rivers” (Sahih Muslim) – a temporary flourish before the final end. But here it refers to the complete devastation. This reality was a stark reminder to the pagan Makkans who took pride in their fertile valleys and trade. Lesson: All earthly attractions are temporary. This knowledge fosters detachment from materialism and focus on the eternal life. It also warns those who are deluded by worldly splendor that it will vanish, and they will stand accountable before Allah on a levelled, barren ground.
verse 9 | part 1 | Have you O Prophet thought that the people of the cave and the plaque were the only wonders of Our signs? | The surah introduces the story of the People of the Cave (Aṣḥāb al-Kahf) and “Ar-Raqīm.” Scholars differ over “Ar-Raqīm”: some say it was a tablet with their names, others say it was the name of the valley or the dog. The question implies that their story, though amazing, is not the only wondrous sign of Allah; there are many greater signs in the heavens and earth. The Prophet was asked by the Quraysh (who had consulted the Jews) about the youths who slept for centuries as a test of his prophethood. Allah revealed their full story. The phrase “Have you thought” (am ḥasibta) addresses the Prophet directly, reassuring him that this knowledge comes from Allah alone. Lesson: Allah’s signs are numerous; no miracle is beyond His power. The story of the cave is a testimony to resurrection and divine protection, but it is just one among countless manifestations of Allah’s might, encouraging believers to reflect on all of creation.
verse 10 | part 1 | Remember when those youths took refuge in the cave | This part marks the beginning of the narrative. The youths were believers who fled to a cave to protect their faith from a polytheistic king. Historical context: Many early Muslim exegetes identify this as occurring in the Christian era before Islam, during the reign of a Roman emperor (like Decius or Diocletian) who persecuted monotheists. These young men were likely among the early Christians who refused to worship idols. Their refuge symbolizes hijrah (emigration) for the sake of faith. The Prophet and his Companions themselves migrated to Madinah for the same reason. The cave became a sanctuary. Lesson: When a believer’s faith is under threat, withdrawing to a safe place is a legitimate and prophetic strategy. Trust in Allah accompanies physical effort.
verse 10 | part 2 | and said, “Our Lord! Grant us mercy from Yourself and guide us rightly through our ordeal.” | This was their supplication in the cave, showing complete reliance on Allah. They asked for raḥmah (mercy) and rashad (guidance) in their difficult situation. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Whoever is anxious or distressed and says ‘O Allah, I hope for Your mercy, so do not leave me to myself even for the blink of an eye, and set right all my affairs,’ Allah will relieve him” (Sunan Abu Dawud). The youths’ prayer was sincere, and Allah answered by granting them the miraculous sleep. They did not ask for wealth or escape, but for mercy and guidance, the most essential things. Lesson: In times of trial, a believer turns to Allah with pure supplication, seeking His mercy and direction rather than merely complaining. The response may come in unexpected forms, like a long sleep that preserved them.
verse 11 | part 1 | So We caused them to fall into a dead sleep in the cave for many years | In answer to their prayer, Allah placed upon their ears a “striking” (ḍarabnā ‘alā ādhānihim) that prevented sound from waking them, resulting in a deep, death-like sleep that lasted 309 lunar years (as later mentioned). The phrase “dead sleep” underscores that it was not ordinary sleep, but a miraculous state suspending biological processes. This is a sign of Allah’s power over life and death. The Prophet (peace be upon him) affirmed the bodily resurrection on the Day of Judgment; this sleep of centuries is a minor example of revival. The years are unspecified in this verse, but the lesson is clear: Allah can preserve His servants beyond normal means. Lesson: Allah’s ability to raise the dead is demonstrated by this event. It comforts the righteous that Allah may protect them in ways humans cannot imagine, and that time is irrelevant to the divine plan.
verse 12 | part 1 | then We raised them so We may show which of the two groups would make a better estimation of the length of their stay. | After many years, Allah woke them. The purpose was to reveal which of the two disputing groups (among the youths themselves, or among the people of the city later) would correctly estimate how long they had slept. Some said a day or part of a day; others acknowledged that only Allah knows. This test of knowledge demonstrated the limitation of human perception. The Prophet commented on disputes about divine matters: “Leave that which makes you doubt for that which does not make you doubt” (Sunan At-Tirmidhi). Lesson: Humans are often inaccurate in judging divine affairs; only Allah knows the complete reality. The best attitude is to humbly concede that Allah’s knowledge encompasses all, and not to argue about matters where proof is lacking.
verse 13 | part 1 | We relate to you O Prophet their story in truth. | Allah affirms that the story being narrated is the absolute truth, not legend or myth. The Quraysh had questioned the Prophet about the cave story, and the Jews had promised that if he answered correctly, he would be a true prophet. The revelation came with precise details. The phrase “in truth” (bil-ḥaqq) assures that the Quranic account is flawless and divinely sourced. The Prophet (peace be upon him) is instructed to recount it with confidence. Lesson: The stories of the Quran are historical facts meant to strengthen faith, not entertainment. Believers should accept them as true and derive lessons from them.
verse 13 | part 2 | They were youths who truly believed in their Lord, and We increased them in guidance. | The youths are praised for their genuine belief. The Arabic “fatiyah” suggests young men, emphasizing that sincere faith is not limited to the old; young people can be deeply devout. Because they believed, Allah increased them in guidance, a divine principle: guidance leads to more guidance. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “There are seven whom Allah will shade on a day when there is no shade but His… a young man who grew up in the worship of Allah” (Sahih Bukhari). Their commitment was rewarded with steadfastness and miraculous protection. Lesson: Youthful faith that stands firm in hostile environments is highly valued. Sincere belief invites divine increase, so believers must constantly seek to strengthen their faith through obedience.
verse 14 | part 1 | And We strengthened their hearts when they stood up and declared | When they publicly proclaimed their faith before the king, Allah fortified their hearts. “Rabaṭnā ‘alā qulūbihim” literally means “We tied up their hearts,” an image of strengthening to prevent fear and weakness. The Prophet often supplicated, “O Turner of hearts, make my heart firm upon Your religion” (Sunan At-Tirmidhi). The historical situation was dangerous: confessing monotheism before a tyrant meant possible execution. But Allah’s support removed their fear. Lesson: Speaking the truth in the face of power requires divine assistance. Believers must ask Allah for a firm heart and then act courageously, knowing that Allah strengthens those who strive for His sake.
verse 14 | part 2 | Our Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth. We will never call upon any god besides Him, or we would truly be uttering an outrageous lie. | This declaration of pure monotheism is their testimony. Acknowledging Allah as Lord of all creation necessitates worshiping Him alone. Calling upon other gods would be an extreme, monstrous falsehood (shaṭaṭan). The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Whoever dies while associating nothing with Allah will enter Paradise” (Sahih Bukhari). This statement directly challenged the polytheistic beliefs of their people, paralleling the message of Prophet Muhammad. Lesson: True faith is to worship the Creator alone, rejecting all false deities. Such conviction may put one in conflict with society, but loyalty to Allah must supersede all.
verse 15 | part 1 | Then they said to one another | The verse shifts to a private conversation among the youths, strategizing after their public stand. This shows that faith also requires prudent planning. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Tie your camel and then trust in Allah” (Sunan At-Tirmidhi). They did not simply rely on miracles; they consulted one another. Lesson: Collective discussion and mutual consultation (shūrā) among believers is important, especially when facing trials. Trust in Allah does not negate taking practical steps.
verse 15 | part 2 | These people of ours have taken gods besides Him. Why do they not produce a clear proof of them? Who then does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah? | They logically refuted idol worship by demanding evidence. Their people’s belief had no clear proof (sulṭān bayyin). The greatest wrongdoer is the one who invents lies about Allah, like claiming that idols are divine. The Prophet said, “The major sins are: associating partners with Allah…” (Sahih Bukhari). The youths used reason to condemn their society’s falsehood, which mirrors the Quran’s own methodology of argument. Lesson: Believers should arm themselves with rational arguments against false beliefs. Fabricating lies about Allah is the peak of injustice, and one must not follow religion based on conjecture.
verse 16 | part 1 | Since you have distanced yourselves from them and what they worship besides Allah, take refuge in the cave. | The youths decided to physically separate themselves from their idolatrous community and seek shelter in the cave. This is a form of hijrah—emigrating with one’s religion when living among disbelievers becomes intolerable. The Prophet said, “I am free from every Muslim who lives among the idolaters” (reported in Sunan Abu Dawud, with a similar command to migrate). Taking refuge in the cave was their chosen means of preservation. Lesson: Sometimes preserving faith requires geographical or social separation from corrupt environments. This hijrah is a renewal of faith, showing that physical sacrifice may be necessary for spiritual survival.
verse 16 | part 2 | Your Lord will extend His mercy to you and accommodate you in your ordeal. | This is a promise of mercy (yanshur lakum) and provision (yuhayyi’) that they gave one another based on trust in Allah. It shows their optimism. The Prophet said, “Strange is the affair of the believer; everything is good for him. If ease comes, he is grateful, and it is good for him. If hardship strikes, he is patient, and it is good for him” (Sahih Muslim). Allah fulfilled their hope by making the cave a comfortable sanctuary. Lesson: When believers take a righteous step, they can confidently expect Allah’s mercy to embrace them and to turn the difficulty into ease. It teaches mutual reassurance and positive thinking based on faith.
verse 17 | part 1 | And you would have seen the sun, as it rose, inclining away from their cave to the right, and as it set, declining away from them to the left, while they lay in its open space. | This vivid description is a miracle: the sun’s rays avoided directly falling on the sleeping youths, preserving their bodies from excessive heat or damage. The cave opening faced a direction that the sun only touched obliquely. The phrase “open space” (fajwah) indicates a spacious interior. This precise astronomical detail is part of the wonder. The Prophet (peace be upon him) did not know this except through revelation. It signifies Allah’s protective care: even the movement of celestial bodies was commanded to serve the preservation of the believers. Lesson: Allah may employ natural phenomena to protect His servants. Nothing in the universe is random; everything obeys Allah’s will. Believers should observe nature as signs of Allah’s meticulous planning.
verse 17 | part 2 | That is one of the signs of Allah. | The entire event—the cave, the sleep, and the sun’s movement—is a sign pointing to Allah’s power and wisdom. It serves as evidence of resurrection, the ability to preserve life for long periods, and the reward for those who uphold faith. In a hadith, “There is no sign among the signs of Allah but that it is a reminder for those who have hearts” (paraphrasing the Quranic idea). Lesson: Miracles are not for mere entertainment; they are lessons to strengthen faith. Each sign calls for reflection on the Creator’s control over everything.
verse 17 | part 3 | Whoever Allah guides is truly guided. But whoever He leaves to stray, you will never find for them a guiding mentor. | This is a profound Quranic principle: true guidance is only from Allah. The Prophet is told that no matter how much he wishes, he cannot guide those whom Allah has left astray. The Prophet said, “Verily, I am only a distributor; Allah is the Bestower” (Sahih Bukhari). His grief over the disbelievers (verse 6) is again addressed. Guidance is tied to sincerity and divine will. Lesson: The caller to truth must depend on Allah for results. Human effort is limited; ultimate guidance is a gift from Allah, so one must constantly pray for it and never be arrogant.
verse 18 | part 1 | And you would have thought they were awake, though they were asleep. | The sleeping youths appeared to be awake because of their open eyes or periodic turning, a supernatural protection to scare off intruders. This was part of their preservation. Allah can make His signs appear in any way He wills. The Prophet (peace be upon him) used to sleep with his right hand under his cheek, but this description is unique to the cave. Lesson: Appearances can be deceiving; reality is determined by Allah. The believers’ state, even when physically asleep, can be a means of awe and protection from enemies.
verse 18 | part 2 | We turned them over, to the right and left, while their dog stretched his forelegs at the entrance. | To prevent bedsores and preserve their bodies, Allah turned them regularly. Even their dog is mentioned, honored for accompanying the righteous. The dog’s posture guarding the entrance added to the intimidating scene. In Islam, a dog used for guarding or hunting is permitted; this story elevates the dog’s loyalty. The Prophet said, “A prostitute was forgiven because she gave water to a thirsty dog” (Sahih Bukhari). Lesson: Sincere association with the pious brings blessings, even to animals. The story highlights the value of loyalty and shows that Allah cares for the smallest details of those He protects.
verse 18 | part 3 | Had you looked at them, you would have certainly fled away from them, filled with horror. | Such was the awe-inspiring state that any onlooker would flee in terror. This prevented discovery. It was a form of divine protection, similar to the fear placed in the hearts of enemies at Badr. The Prophet said, “I was aided with terror (cast into the hearts of enemies) for a distance of a month’s journey” (Sahih Bukhari). This psychological deterrence kept the cave unentered for centuries. Lesson: Allah employs various means to safeguard His faithful. Sometimes protection comes through fear, not physical barriers, showing the power of the unseen.
verse 19 | part 1 | And so We awakened them so that they might question one another. | At the appointed time, Allah raised them from sleep. Upon waking, they naturally began questioning each other about how long they had slept. This interaction was part of the sign for the people of the city and for the youths themselves to realize the miracle. The Prophet said, “Whoever wakes up safely in his home, healthy in body, and has food for his day, it is as if the world has been collected for him” (Sunan At-Tirmidhi). Lesson: Awakening each day is a blessing; believers should use their consciousness to acknowledge Allah’s power and to inquire into the lessons of life.
verse 19 | part 2 | One of them exclaimed, “How long have you remained asleep?” Some replied, “Perhaps a day, or part of a day.” | Their guess reflected the normal human perception of a brief sleep, showing they did not know the centuries that passed. This underestimation underscores the limits of human knowledge. Similarly, on the Day of Resurrection, disbelievers will think they stayed only a short while (20:103). Lesson: Time is relative, and human sense of it is limited. Only Allah knows the real duration of states. This reminds people to value the time they have, as their perception may differ from reality.
verse 19 | part 3 | They said to one another, “Your Lord knows best how long you have remained. So send one of you with these silver coins of yours to the city, and let him find which food is the purest, and then bring you provisions from it. Let him be exceptionally cautious, and do not let him give you away.” | After acknowledging Allah’s perfect knowledge, they focused on practical needs. They sent a member with their original silver coins (indicating they were from a time when those coins were current, showing Allah preserved their wealth too). “Purest” (azkā) food means lawful and wholesome. Extreme caution was required to avoid detection by the tyrant’s regime. This shows a balance of reliance on Allah and worldly prudence. The Prophet said, “Seeking the lawful is an obligation after the obligatory acts” (Sunan Ibn Majah). Lesson: Even in miraculous situations, believers must take necessary precautions and seek lawful provision. Trust in Allah does not negate careful planning.
verse 20 | part 1 | For, indeed, if they find out about you, they will stone you to death, or force you back into their faith, and then you will never succeed. | The youths warned each other of the consequences: execution by stoning or forced apostasy. This reflects the historic persecution of monotheists. If they returned to the pagan faith, they would lose both this world and the Hereafter. The Quran warns against apostasy: “Whoever among you reverts from his religion… their deeds become worthless” (2:217). The Prophet said, “Whoever changes his religion, kill him” (Sahih Bukhari) in the context of apostasy as a capital crime in an Islamic state. Lesson: Compromising faith under threat leads to eternal loss. Believers must value their religion above their lives, as true success lies in remaining steadfast until death.
verse 21 | part 1 | That is how We caused them to be discovered so that their people might know that Allah’s promise of resurrection is true and that there is no doubt about the Hour. | Allah arranged their discovery to prove the truth of resurrection. The people of the city were disputing whether bodily resurrection was possible. Seeing these youths alive after centuries was undeniable evidence. The Prophet said, “People will be resurrected barefoot, naked, and uncircumcised” (Sahih Bukhari). The event removed all doubt about the Hour. Lesson: Allah provides tangible proofs for those who reflect. The miracle of the cave is a clear sign that just as Allah preserved them, He can resurrect all humanity.
verse 21 | part 2 | When the people disputed with each other about the case of the youth after their death, some proposed, “Build a structure around them. Their Lord knows best about them.” | After the youths died (or when their situation was discovered), the people argued. One group suggested building a structure over the cave, possibly as a memorial or to seal it. The comment “Their Lord knows best about them” was a recognition that the full truth was with Allah. The Prophet said, “Do not sit on graves and do not pray facing them” (Sahih Muslim) – indicating caution about structures over graves. Lesson: Disputes over religious matters often arise even after clear signs; the best response is to defer ultimate knowledge to Allah and avoid excessive veneration of the dead.
verse 21 | part 3 | Those who prevailed in the matter said, “We will surely build a place of worship over them.” | The dominant group decided to construct a masjid (place of worship) over the site. This is seen by many scholars as a condemned action if it leads to grave worship. The Prophet said, “Allah cursed the Jews and Christians who took the graves of their prophets as places of worship” (Sahih Bukhari). This verse warns against such practices. Historically, this refers to the victors in the dispute, who were perhaps the religious authorities of the time, turning the event into a shrine. Lesson: Monotheism must be guarded against exaggeration; building mosques over graves risks idolatry. True faith keeps worship directed to Allah alone, not to saints or their relics.
verse 22 | part 1 | Some will say, “They were three, their dog was the fourth,” while others will say, “They were five, their dog was the sixth,” guessing blindly. | The Quran reports people’s conjectures about the exact number of the sleepers. The first group says three plus a dog, the second says five plus a dog. The phrase “rajman bil-ghayb” (guessing blindly) denounces such speculation without knowledge. The Prophet said, “Beware of suspicion, for indeed it is the most false of speech” (Sahih Bukhari). These guesses were likely from Christians or others who debated the story. Lesson: Speculating about unknown details without evidence is futile. It leads to divisions and distracts from the real lessons. Believers should only speak with certain knowledge.
verse 22 | part 2 | And others will say, “They were seven and their dog was the eighth.” Say, O Prophet, “My Lord knows best their exact number. Only a few people know as well.” | The third opinion suggests seven plus one dog. Allah commands the Prophet to respond that Allah alone knows the exact number, and only a few people have been given that knowledge (perhaps the People of the Book who had authentic revelation, but whose knowledge is now corrupted). This directs the focus back to Allah’s omniscience. The lesson of the story is not the number but the signs of resurrection and protection. Lesson: The precise number is irrelevant to faith. What matters is acknowledging Allah’s knowledge and focusing on the moral teachings. Insisting on peripheral details breeds unnecessary argument.
verse 22 | part 3 | So do not argue about them except with sure knowledge, nor consult any of those who debate about them. | The command forbids deep argument (jidal) without firm evidence, and prohibits consulting those who argue aimlessly (the People of the Book who would dispute). The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “I guarantee a house in the outskirts of Paradise for the one who abandons arguing even if he is right” (Sunan Abu Dawud). This was crucial for the Muslim community to avoid fruitless debates with Jews and Christians about the cave story. Lesson: Muslims must not engage in speculative disputes about unclear matters. Preserving peace and focusing on clear guidance is better than winning arguments. Humble submission to Allah’s knowledge protects the unity of the heart and community.
verse 23 | part 1 | And never say of anything, “I will definitely do this tomorrow,” | This verse instructs believers never to speak with absolute certainty about future actions without linking them to Allah’s will. The phrase “never say” is a strong prohibition, teaching that even the firmest human intention is contingent upon Allah’s decree. Historically, this verse was revealed when the Quraysh asked the Prophet (peace be upon him) three questions, including the story of the People of the Cave. He replied, “Come back tomorrow and I will inform you,” but he forgot to add “if Allah wills” (Inshā’ Allāh). As a result, revelation ceased for fifteen days, causing distress and mockery from the disbelievers. Then this verse came down to correct and guide. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The example of a believer who recites the Quran and acts upon it is like a citron, which tastes good and smells good; and the believer who does not act upon it is like a date, which has no good smell but tastes sweet” (Sahih Bukhari). The lesson is that acknowledging Allah’s overriding power over all future events is a mark of true servitude. Humans plan, but Allah’s will ultimately determines outcomes. Therefore, a Muslim’s speech must always reflect this dependency, not out of empty habit, but from deep conviction. This practice protects against arrogance, self-delusion, and the disappointment of unfulfilled plans, reminding one that the future belongs solely to Allah.
verse 24 | part 1 | without adding, “if Allah so wills!” | The phrase “if Allah so wills” (Inshā’ Allāh) is the verbal declaration of reliance on Allah’s will. The Arabic word “shā’a” means to will, so it means “if Allah wills.” The verse explicitly commands appending this to any statement about future action. Historically, this practice became obligatory after the above-mentioned incident, and it remains an essential etiquette of a Muslim. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “If any one of you swears an oath and says ‘if Allah wills’ then he has made an exception, and it will not be considered a broken oath” (Sunan Abu Dawud). This teaches that the phrase is not merely a ritual but a legal and spiritual safety net. It acknowledges that no human power can compel an event; Allah alone causes it. The pagan Arabs had no such concept; they boasted of their own strength. This verse established a distinctive Islamic worldview: all future occurrences are possible only by the will of Allah. Even scientific certainties, like the sunrise, are sustained moment by moment by Allah’s will. Therefore, a believer must never arrogantly assume control over tomorrow. This is a profound lesson in humility, monotheism, and the constant recognition that Allah is Al-Qādir (the All-Powerful).
verse 24 | part 2 | But if you forget, then remember your Lord, and say, “I trust my Lord will guide me to what is more right than this.” | This part offers a remedy for human forgetfulness. If a believer forgets to say “Inshā’ Allāh,” they should, upon remembering, immediately turn to Allah and say a supplication trusting that Allah will guide them to something better. The phrase “more right” (rushdā) implies guidance to the most correct or beneficial outcome. The Prophet (peace be upon him) himself, after the revelation was delayed, was taught to say this. It teaches that forgetting is natural, but the remedy is immediate remembrance of Allah. The hadith says, “When a man forgets something, he should remember Allah; for remembering Allah removes forgetfulness” (paraphrase, similar to hadith in Sunan At-Tirmidhi). This instruction lifts despair over past oversights: one should not dwell on the mistake but reconnect with Allah, expressing trust that He will turn the situation into one of better guidance. It fosters an optimistic, Allah-centered mindset. Historical context: after this verse, the Prophet would regularly say “Inshā’ Allāh” and taught his Companions to do the same. They began their letters, treaties, and plans with it. Thus, a simple phrase transformed their entire approach to time and action, rooting their culture in monotheistic humility.
verse 25 | part 1 | They had remained in their cave for three hundred years, adding nine. | This verse settles the exact duration of the sleep of the People of the Cave. The Arabic states “three hundred (solar) years, and they added nine” (which makes 309 lunar years). This precise number resolves all speculation from verse 22. Historically, some interpreters note that 300 solar years equal exactly 309 lunar years, showing the Quran’s precision across calendars. This miracle reminds us that Allah records time accurately, and the sleepers’ long slumber is a sign of His power. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The nations were shown to me, and I saw a prophet with a small group, and a prophet with one or two men, and a prophet with none. Then a huge crowd appeared and I said, ‘What is this? Are these my followers?’ It was said, ‘No, but look at the horizon.’ I saw a huge crowd, and it was said, ‘These are your followers, and among them are seventy thousand who will enter Paradise without reckoning.’” The mention of the exact years underlines that no detail escapes Allah’s knowledge. Lesson: The story is historical fact, not myth. The precise duration demonstrates that the Quran is a book of truth, with no approximation in matters of the unseen. It also reminds believers that time for human beings is measurable, but Allah is beyond time.
verse 26 | part 1 | Say, O Prophet, “Allah knows best how long they stayed. With Him alone is the knowledge of the unseen of the heavens and the earth.” | When the exact number of years was revealed, the Prophet was still commanded to declare that ultimate knowledge belongs to Allah alone. This emphasizes that while Allah shares some unseen knowledge, the complete unseen is exclusively His. The phrase “Allah knows best” (Allāhu a‘lam) is a recurring Quranic command, teaching humility. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The keys of the unseen are five, which no one knows except Allah: none knows what is in the wombs except Allah, none knows what will happen tomorrow except Allah, none knows when rain will come except Allah, no soul knows in which land it will die except Allah, and none knows when the Hour will be established except Allah” (Sahih Bukhari). The People of the Cave’s sleep is part of the unseen; only through revelation was it known. Lesson: Muslims must refrain from speaking about the unseen without divine sanction. This guards against superstition and false claims. It also fosters complete reliance on Allah, who is the Knower of the invisible and visible.
verse 26 | part 2 | How perfectly He hears and sees! They have no guardian besides Him, and He shares His command with none. | This praises Allah’s absolute perfection in hearing and seeing. Unlike created beings, His hearing encompasses all sounds, whispers, and even inner thoughts; His seeing encompasses all that is visible or hidden, in darkness or light. The phrase “How perfectly He hears and sees!” is an exclamation of wonder at His attributes. Following that, it declares that humans have no true guardian (walī) other than Allah, and that He does not share His decree or authority with any partner. Historically, the pagan Arabs turned to idols and intercessors for protection and decision-making. This verse utterly negates such ideas, affirming monotheism. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “If you ask, ask of Allah; if you seek help, seek help of Allah” (Sunan At-Tirmidhi). The lesson is that believers must direct all worship, hope, and fear to Allah alone. No saint, angel, or prophet shares in His rule. His hearing and seeing assure justice and mercy for His servants.
verse 27 | part 1 | Recite what has been revealed to you from the Book of your Lord. None can change His Words, nor can you find any refuge besides Him. | The command to “recite” (wa-atlu) means to convey, read, and follow the Quran that was revealed. The Prophet is told to persist in his mission, as the revelation is unchangeable. “None can change His Words” is a promise of preservation: no human or jinn can alter the Quran. This gave the Prophet (peace be upon him) confidence against the disbelievers who sought to corrupt or silence the message. The Prophet said, “This knowledge will be carried by the trustworthy ones of each generation, who will remove from it the distortions of the extremists, the false claims of the liars, and the misinterpretations of the ignorant” (a well-known narration). The phrase “nor can you find any refuge besides Him” emphasizes that true protection is only with Allah. If the Prophet or any believer abandons the Quran, they have no other shelter. Lesson: The divine word is unalterable and provides ultimate security. Muslims must cling to the Quran, knowing its message is preserved and sufficient as a refuge against falsehood.
verse 28 | part 1 | And patiently stick with those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His pleasure. | The Prophet is instructed to keep company with the sincere, humble believers who worship Allah constantly, not desiring worldly prestige. Historical context: Some Quraysh chiefs told the Prophet they would listen to him only if he sent away the poor and low-status Muslims like Bilal, Suhayb, and Khabbab. They wanted a religion that catered to the elite. Allah rebuked this attitude, commanding the Prophet to stick with those sincere worshippers regardless of social status. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Allah does not look at your outward appearance or wealth, but He looks at your hearts and your deeds” (Sahih Muslim). “Morning and evening” signifies consistency in worship. Lesson: True believers are known by their constant devotion and sincerity, not by wealth or rank. The community of faith must be built on piety, not social discrimination. Leaders should prioritize the poor but pious believers, never rejecting them for worldly gain.
verse 28 | part 2 | Do not let your eyes look beyond them, desiring the luxuries of this worldly life. | This means the Prophet (and by extension every believer) should not disregard humble believers in favor of worldly glitter. The phrase “let your eyes look beyond them” (lā ta‘du ‘aynāka ‘anhum) literally means “do not let your eyes pass them over,” indicating contempt or neglect. “Luxuries of this worldly life” refers to the adornments that delude people. In a hadith, the Prophet said, “Look to those who are lower than you (in worldly matters) and do not look to those who are above you, lest you belittle the favors of Allah upon you” (Sahih Muslim). The Quraysh elite’s desire for an exclusive gathering was a reflection of their arrogance. This verse teaches that a believer’s standard is faith, not fashion. Lesson: believers must guard their hearts from envy and materialism, valuing the company of the righteous above the glamour of the worldly.
verse 28 | part 3 | And do not obey those whose hearts We have made heedless of Our remembrance, who follow only their desires and whose state is total loss. | The command forbids obeying the heedless disbelievers. Such people have hearts that are neglectful of Allah’s remembrance—they do not care about divine guidance. They follow their own whims (hawā), making their desires their god. The result is utter ruin (furuṭā). The Prophet (peace be upon him) warned, “Beware of blindly following desires, for it leads astray from the path of Allah” (similar meaning). Historically, the Meccan chiefs were epitomized here: wealthy, powerful, but spiritually dead, pressing the Prophet to compromise. Lesson: A believer cannot submit to the demands of those whose hearts are disconnected from Allah, no matter how influential they are. Obedience is only due in what is right and in line with revelation. True success lies in remembrance of Allah, not in following the temporal trends of the heedless.
verse 29 | part 1 | And say, O Prophet, “This is the truth from your Lord. Whoever wills let them believe, and whoever wills let them disbelieve.” | The Prophet is told to present the message and leave the choice to individuals, as belief cannot be forced. The statement is not a license to disbelieve; it is a warning that people will bear the consequences of their own choice. The truth is clearly declared, and after that, human free will is respected. This echoes “There is no compulsion in religion” (2:256). The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “By Allah, if they disbelieve, they will face destruction, but my mission is only to convey clearly” (paraphrase). The consequence of disbelief follows immediately. Lesson: Da‘wah (calling to Islam) is about conveying the truth, not about forcing results. The choice is solemn, and each soul is accountable.
verse 29 | part 2 | Surely We have prepared for the wrongdoers a Fire whose walls will completely surround them. | The “wrongdoers” are those who reject the truth. The Fire is described as a “surādiq” – a canopy or enclosed pavilion – meaning the Hellfire surrounds them from all sides, offering no escape. In a hadith, the Prophet described Hell: “Its heat is intense, its depth is immense, and it is enclosed by walls of fire” (part of a longer narration). This imagery emphasizes total containment and punishment. The Quraysh, who mocked the idea of a physical hell, are warned of its imminent reality. Lesson: The torment is real, severe, and inescapable for those who persist in wrongdoing. This should instill a healthy fear that motivates repentance and righteousness.
verse 29 | part 3 | When they cry for aid, they will be aided with water like molten metal, which will burn their faces. What a horrible drink! And what a terrible place to rest! | The punishment’s details are horrific. When they seek relief from thirst, they are given water like “muhl”— molten metal or thick oil that scalds. Instead of cooling, it burns their faces. The Prophet said in Sahih Bukhari, “Hell complained to its Lord, saying, ‘O my Lord, parts of me have devoured each other.’ So He permitted it to take two breaths: one breath in winter and one breath in summer. The severe heat you experience is from its breath, and the severe cold is from its breath.” The drink is described as terrible (bi’sa ash-sharāb) and the resting place as miserable. This is a stark warning to disbelievers who were comfortable in their worldly luxuries. Lesson: Worldly comforts are fleeting; the true horror is the eternal punishment. This verse serves as a deterrent, pushing believers to seek Allah’s mercy and avoid actions that lead to such a fate.
verse 30 | part 1 | As for those who believe and do good, We certainly never deny the reward of those who are best in deeds. | After the warning comes the glad tidings. Those who combine faith (īmān) and righteous deeds (ṣāliḥāt) are assured that their reward will never be lost or diminished. “We certainly never deny” (lā nuḍī‘u) is a strong affirmation from Allah that He preserves every good deed, no matter how small. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Fear Allah, and do not belittle any good deed, even meeting your brother with a cheerful face” (Sahih Muslim). The phrase “best in deeds” (aḥsanu ‘amalan) points to the quality and sincerity of actions, not just quantity. This verse was a tremendous encouragement for the poor Companions mentioned in the previous passages; their good deeds were valued immensely. Lesson: Divine justice is perfect. Not an atom’s weight of good is wasted. This should encourage believers to excel in their actions, knowing that Allah sees and will reward them fully.
verse 31 | part 1 | It is they who will have the Gardens of Eternity, with rivers flowing under their feet. | The reward is detailed: eternal gardens (Jannātu ‘Adn) with flowing rivers beneath their palaces. “Eternity” means no death, no ending. The image of flowing water was particularly appealing to the desert Arabs of Makkah, who lived in arid conditions. The Prophet (peace be upon him) described Paradise: “In Paradise there are one hundred levels, prepared by Allah for the mujahideen in His cause, the distance between each level being like that between the heaven and the earth” (Sahih Bukhari). This promise gave hope to the persecuted early Muslims. Lesson: The ultimate home of the righteous is a place of everlasting peace, beauty, and satisfaction, far beyond any worldly comfort. It calls believers to work for that eternal abode.
verse 31 | part 2 | There they will be adorned with bracelets of gold, and wear green garments of fine silk and rich brocade, reclining there on canopied couches. | The luxury described includes gold bracelets, green silk garments, brocade (sundus and istabraq), and elevated couches. Such adornments were symbols of royalty in the ancient world. In a hadith, the Prophet said, “The first group to enter Paradise will be like the moon on a full-moon night, and those who follow them will be like the brightest planet. They will not urinate, relieve nature, spit, or have any nasal secretions. Their combs will be of gold, and their sweat will smell like musk” (Sahih Bukhari). The green garments indicate freshness and honor. The reclining posture denotes total ease and no worry. Lesson: Worldly luxuries sought by disbelievers are nothing compared to Paradise’s delights. Allah will honor the faithful with unimaginable splendor, rewarding their patience in poverty or simplicity.
verse 31 | part 3 | What a marvellous reward! And what a fabulous place to rest! | The verse ends with divine admiration of this reward: “ni‘ma ath-thawāb” (excellent reward) and “ḥasunat murtafaqā” (fine resting place). This is Allah praising His own generosity, urging believers to yearn for it. The resting place (mutarfaqā) implies a place of leisure and hospitality, where all needs are met. It contrasts with the “terrible place to rest” of the Hellfire in verse 29. The lesson is clear: the choice between two destinations is stark. It should evoke hope in the hearts of the believers and prompt them to remain steadfast.
verse 32 | part 1 | Give them O Prophet an example of two men. To the disbelieving one We gave two gardens of grapevines, which We surrounded with palm trees and placed various crops in between. | The surah transitions to the story of the two men. One was given two lush gardens of grapevines, surrounded by date palms and interspersed with crops. This represents extreme worldly wealth. The gardens are irrigated and self-contained ecosystems. Historically, Makkans like ‘Uyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn or others might have been the archetype, but the story is a parable. The man is “disbelieving” (kāfir), meaning ungrateful. The Prophet said, “This world is the prison of the believer and the paradise of the disbeliever” (Sahih Muslim). The abundance is a test, not a sign of divine favor. Lesson: Wealth is a trial; its presence does not indicate Allah’s pleasure, nor its absence His displeasure. The believer must be cautious with riches, recognizing they are a trust.
verse 33 | part 1 | Each garden yielded all its produce, never falling short. And We caused a river to flow between them. | The productivity of the gardens was perfect, with no failure. A river flowed constantly, providing irrigation, beauty, and luxury. This ideal setting resembled the fertile valleys of Ta’if or Damascus. The Quran paints a picture of peak worldly success to contrast with the barren end. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “If the son of Adam had a valley of gold, he would desire another, and nothing will fill his mouth except the dust of the grave” (Sahih Bukhari). The gardens’ perfection masked the man’s spiritual poverty. Lesson: Material abundance without faith is a trap. No matter how perfect the worldly means, they can vanish instantly. The believer should value the “river” of faith over the rivers of worldly gain.
verse 34 | part 1 | And he had other resources as well. So he boasted to a poor companion of his, while conversing with him, “I am greater than you in wealth and superior in manpower.” | Besides the gardens, the man possessed other thamar (fruits or profits, interpreted as gold, silver, or servants). Arrogance led him to boast to his believing companion, who was poor in worldly terms. The claim “I am greater than you in wealth and superior in manpower” reflects pride in material assets and social connections (family, guards). Historically, the pagans of Makkah similarly boasted of their wealth and clan strength over the Muslims. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Allah has revealed to me that you should be humble, so that no one boasts over another, and no one wrongs another” (Sahih Muslim). Lesson: Boasting about worldly possessions is a sign of spiritual sickness. True honor is in piety, not in money or followers.
verse 35 | part 1 | And he entered his property, while wronging his soul, saying, “I do not think this will ever perish,” | The disbeliever enters his garden arrogantly, committing wrong against himself. He expresses disbelief in the transience of his wealth. This is denial of reality; all worldly things perish. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The son of Adam says, ‘My property, my property,’ but truly, your property is only what you consume and have thus destroyed, or what you wear and have worn out, or what you give in charity and have sent ahead” (Sahih Muslim). The statement reflects a secular, materialist mindset that denies accountability. Lesson: Arrogance blinds one to the reality of death and loss. Believers must remember that everything on earth is temporary and that hoarding without gratitude leads to self-destruction.
verse 36 | part 1 | nor do I think the Hour will ever come. And if in fact I am returned to my Lord, I will definitely get a far better outcome than all this. | He expresses doubt about the Day of Judgment, and even if it happens, he arrogantly assumes he deserves something better there too. This is twofold foolishness: denial of resurrection and false hope. The Quran criticizes such wishful thinking: “That is their wishful thinking” (4:123). The pagan Arabs, especially the wealthy, held similar views. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “A person who says, ‘People are ruined,’ is himself the most ruined among them” (Sahih Muslim). The man’s statement reflects ingratitude and self-deception. Lesson: Wealth can create a false sense of security and entitlement, even towards Allah. True faith requires acknowledging that this life is a test and that the Hereafter’s rewards are based on faith and deeds, not on worldly status.
verse 37 | part 1 | His believing companion replied, while conversing with him, “Do you disbelieve in the One Who created you from dust, then developed you from a sperm-drop, then formed you into a man?” | The poor believer responds with reason, reminding him of his humble origin—dust and then a drop of sperm. This is a call to humility: if Allah created him from such lowly substances, how can he boast and deny the Creator? The Quran repeatedly uses this argument. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “There is no one who is born except that he touches the dust (i.e., is created from dust and will return to dust)” (paraphrase). The stages of creation are signs of Allah’s power and wisdom. The believer is engaging in da‘wah with wisdom, not responding with insults. Lesson: The correct response to arrogance is to invite reflection on one’s origins, fostering humility and gratitude. The argument from human creation is one of the strongest proofs of Allah’s existence and power.
verse 38 | part 1 | But as for me: He is Allah, my Lord, and I will never associate anyone with my Lord in worship. | The believing man declares his pure monotheism, contrasting with his companion’s de facto shirk of self-worship and denial. “He is Allah, my Lord” is a confession of faith. He associates nothing with Allah, whether wealth, status, or other gods. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Whoever dies without having associated anything with Allah will enter Paradise, and whoever dies associating something with Allah will enter the Fire” (Sahih Muslim). The believer’s identity is grounded in tawḥīd. Lesson: True wealth is the wealth of faith. Regardless of material poverty, a believer possesses the greatest treasure: recognition of Allah’s oneness and a pure relationship with Him. This confession is the ultimate dignity.
verse 39 | part 1 | If only you had said, upon entering your property, ‘This is what Allah has willed! There is no power except with Allah!’ | The believer advises what should have been said: “Mā shā’ Allāh, lā quwwata illā billāh.” This is the Islamic etiquette upon seeing one’s blessings: attribute them to Allah’s will and deny any personal power independent of Him. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Whoever is granted a blessing and says, ‘Praise be to Allah’ and ‘Mā shā’ Allāh’ in acknowledgment, it will be blessed for him” (paraphrase). The wealthy man’s failure to say this led to arrogance and destruction. This supplication is a shield against envy and self-conceit. Lesson: Recognizing that all blessings are from Allah’s will and that human strength is only through Him protects the heart from pride and invites divine preservation. It is a simple yet profound practice to implement daily.
verse 39 | part 2 | Even though you see me inferior to you in wealth and offspring, | The believing man acknowledges his outward inferiority in material things and children, yet he does not feel inferior. Status in the sight of Allah is not based on these. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “No one is better than anyone else except by piety” (a principle from the Quran and Sunnah). The companion’s contentment despite poverty is a sign of deep faith. Lesson: Societal standards of success are false. A believer’s self-worth comes from faith and character, not from wealth or progeny. This liberates one from envy and competition over dunyā.
verse 40 | part 1 | perhaps my Lord will grant me something better than your garden, and send down upon your garden a thunderbolt from the sky, turning it into a barren waste. | The believer expresses hope in Allah’s ability to give him something better while also warning of the possible destruction of the arrogant man’s garden by a ḥusbān (a calamity or thunderbolt). This is not envy but a statement of fact about divine power. The Prophet (peace be upon him) warned that nations were destroyed for their ingratitude and sins. The believer’s hope is placed in Allah, not in the material world. Lesson: The faithful do not gloat over the worldly gains of evildoers; they entrust matters to Allah, knowing He can replace any blessing and punish ingratitude suddenly. The true treasure is divine reward, which is secure.
verse 41 | part 1 | Or its water may sink into the earth, and then you will never be able to seek it out. | Another possible destruction: the garden’s water source could disappear into the deep earth (ghawrā), making recovery impossible. Water was the lifeline; without it, the lush gardens would die. This shows how fragile worldly resources are. Allah says elsewhere, “Say, ‘Have you considered: if your water was to become sunken [into the earth], then who could bring you flowing water?’” (67:30). The arrogance of the owner assumed permanent abundance. Lesson: Every blessing is sustained by Allah moment by moment. Loss of the most basic necessities can happen in an instant, so no one should feel secure in their provision without gratitude and obedience.
verse 42 | part 1 | And so all his produce was totally ruined, so he started to wring his hands for all he had spent on it, while it had collapsed on its trellises. | The punishment arrived: his fruits and garden were destroyed, likely by a storm or blight. The trellises collapsed, and his investment was lost. He began wringing his hands in regret—a gesture of extreme anguish and remorse. The phrase “akhadha yuqallibu kaffayhi” depicts bitter retrospection. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Regret is a form of repentance” (Sunan Ibn Majah), but this man’s regret was over lost wealth, not over sins. Lesson: Worldly pursuits without faith end in ruin and regret. The scene is a powerful metaphor for those who build their lives on materialism: when divine punishment comes, everything collapses, and no amount of remorse can restore what is lost.
verse 42 | part 2 | He cried, “Alas! I wish I had never associated anyone with my Lord in worship!” | In his despair, he finally acknowledges his wrongdoing: shirk (associating partners with Allah), which in his case was his attachment to wealth and his denial of the Hereafter. Yet this recognition came too late. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Accepting faith at the time of death or punishment is not accepted” (as per the story of Pharaoh). His utterance is one of loss, not of sincere repentance. Lesson: Delaying repentance until calamity strikes is futile. True faith requires turning to Allah before it is too late. The statement also confirms that attributing power to other than Allah (including one’s own self) is a form of shirk.
verse 43 | part 1 | And he had no manpower to help him against Allah, nor could he even help himself. | The “manpower” he boasted about earlier (verse 34) was absent. No army, clan, or servants could protect him from Allah’s decree. He could not defend himself either (mā kāna muntaṣirā). This illustrates the ultimate helplessness of a soul facing divine punishment. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “O people, you will not be asked about your lineage on the Day of Judgment; the most honored of you is the most pious” (part of the Farewell Sermon). Lesson: All worldly supports vanish in the face of Allah’s will. Only faith and righteous deeds remain. This humbles every person and calls for reliance on Allah alone.
verse 44 | part 1 | At this time, support comes only from Allah—the True Lord. He is best in reward and best in outcome. | The final commentary on the story: true protection and guardianship (al-walāyah) belong only to Allah, the True God. He is the best in rewarding and in securing a good final end. “Best in outcome” (‘uqbā) encompasses both the worldly consequence and the Hereafter. The Prophet (peace be upon him) would often supplicate: “O Allah, I ask You for a good end in all my affairs, and I seek refuge in You from the disgrace of this world and the punishment of the Hereafter” (Musnad Ahmad). This story’s lesson is encapsulated here: ultimate power, reward, and salvation are from Allah alone, so believers must dedicate their lives to Him and not be deceived by fleeting worldly wealth.
verse 45 | part 1 | And give them a parable of this worldly life. It is like the plants of the earth, thriving when sustained by the rain We send down from the sky. Then they soon turn into chaff scattered by the wind. | A new parable: the life of this world is like a plant that flourishes after rain but quickly becomes dry stubble (hashīm) blown away by winds. This simile illustrates the transience of worldly beauty and prosperity. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “This world compared to the Hereafter is like dipping your finger in the sea and seeing what clings to it” (Sahih Muslim). Historically, the Makkans were proud of their trade and fertile seasons. This reminds them that everything ends. Lesson: Do not be deluded by the temporary splendor. Invest in what lasts: good deeds and remembrance of Allah. The imagery calls for detachment from materialism.
verse 45 | part 2 | And Allah is fully capable of doing all things. | This concluding phrase (wa kāna Allāhu ‘alā kulli shay’in muqtadirā) emphasizes that Allah has absolute power to give life and death, to bring forth and to destroy. His ability extends to resurrection and judgment. It ties the entire discourse together: the People of the Cave’s preservation, the destruction of the garden, and the transformation of the world. Nothing is beyond His capability. Lesson: Trust in Allah’s power is the foundation of faith. The believer is at peace knowing that the Controller of everything manages their affairs, and no power can thwart His will.
verse 46 | part 1 | Wealth and children are the adornment of this worldly life, but the everlasting good deeds are far better with your Lord in reward and in hope. | The verse explicitly states that wealth and children are the “adornment” (zīnah) of the dunyā—not inherently evil, but the beautification and test. However, “everlasting good deeds” (al-bāqiyāt aṣ-ṣāliḥāt) are far superior. These include the five daily prayers, remembrance of Allah (subḥān Allāh, al-ḥamdu lillāh, lā ilāha illā Allāh, Allāhu akbar), charity, and any action done for Allah’s sake. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Shall I tell you about the best of your deeds, the purest in the sight of your Lord… it is remembrance of Allah” (Sunan At-Tirmidhi). “Better in reward” and “in hope” indicates that these deeds yield true, lasting benefit and are what a believer truly places hope in, not fleeting possessions. Lesson: Prioritize the eternal over the temporary. Wealth and family are trusts that must not distract from the ultimate goal. The only profitable investment is that which remains after death.
verse 47 | part 1 | Beware of the Day We will blow the mountains away, and you will see the earth laid bare. And We will gather all humankind, leaving none behind. | This scene depicts the Day of Judgment. The mountains, symbols of firmness and stability, will be blown away (nusayyiru al-jibāl) or set in motion and crushed. The earth will become a flat, clear plain (bārizah), with no hiding places. Allah will gather every single human being, leaving no one (lam nughādir). The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “On the Day of Resurrection, people will be gathered barefoot, naked, and uncircumcised” (Sahih Bukhari). This is a terrifying portrayal of complete exposure and accountability. Lesson: The Day of Judgment is an undeniable reality that levels all worldly distinctions. Believers must live with this awareness, preparing for the moment of gathering where nothing is hidden.
verse 48 | part 1 | They will be presented before your Lord in rows, and the deniers will be told, “You have surely returned to Us all alone as We created you the first time, although you always claimed that We would never appoint a time for your return.” | The people are arranged in orderly rows (ṣaffan) before Allah. The disbelievers who denied resurrection are confronted: they have returned to Allah alone, stripped of wealth, family, and power, just as they were first created. This is a fulfillment of the warning. They used to claim no meeting would occur, but now it is reality. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Every servant will stand before Allah on the Day of Judgment, and Allah will speak to him directly, with no interpreter” (Sahih Bukhari). The humiliation of their false assertions is complete. Lesson: Denial does not change truth. All souls must stand before their Creator, so a wise person lives conscious of this meeting and prepares for it with belief and good deeds.
verse 49 | part 1 | And the record of deeds will be laid open, and you will see the wicked in fear of what is written in it. They will cry, “Woe to us! What kind of record is this that does not leave any sin, small or large, unlisted?” | The book of each person’s deeds is placed openly, causing criminals to tremble. They are astonished that every sin, no matter how trivial or serious (ṣaghīrah or kabīrah), is recorded exactly, without omission. This demonstrates divine precision and justice. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Beware of minor sins, for they gather upon a man until they destroy him, like a people who camped in a valley and each brought a stick until they cooked their bread” (Musnad Ahmad). The record leaves nothing unaccounted, which terrifies those who were heedless. Lesson: No deed is insignificant; all are registered. This should make believers conscientious in even the smallest matters, avoiding sins and doing good, knowing that everything is seen and preserved.
verse 49 | part 2 | They will find whatever they did present before them. And your Lord will never wrong anyone. | All actions, good or bad, will be present in front of them as witness. The phrase “wajadū mā ‘amilū ḥāḍirā” means they will face the consequences of their deeds. The final sentence “And your Lord will never wrong anyone” (lā yaẓlimu rabbuka aḥadā) reaffirms the absolute justice of Allah. No one will be punished more than deserved, and no good will be overlooked. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Allah said: O My servants, I have forbidden injustice for Myself and made it forbidden among you, so do not wrong one another” (Sahih Muslim). This verse offers comfort to the oppressed and a warning to the oppressor. Lesson: Perfect justice awaits; no grievance will be ignored. Believers can leave matters to Allah, trusting in His fairness. It is a call to do justice in this life, reflecting the Lord’s attribute of justice, and to seek His mercy for shortcomings.
verse 50 | part 1 | And remember when We said to the angels, “Prostrate before Adam,” so they all did—but not Iblîs, who was one of the jinn, but he rebelled against the command of his Lord. | This verse recalls the primordial event when Allah commanded the angels to prostrate to Adam as a sign of honor, not worship. All angels complied except Iblīs (Satan). The Quran clarifies that Iblīs was not an angel but from the jinn, a being created from smokeless fire, possessing free will. His refusal was the first act of arrogance and disobedience against Allah. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The angels were created from light, the jinn from a smokeless flame of fire, and Adam from what has been described to you” (Sahih Muslim). Historically, this story establishes the root of enmity between humans and Satan. Iblīs’s rebellion stemmed from pride: he considered himself superior to Adam because of his origin from fire versus clay. This is the first sin: arrogance before Allah’s command. Allah expelled him, and he became the avowed enemy of humankind. The prostration commanded was not an act of worship but of respect, lawful in previous laws. In Islam, prostration to anyone other than Allah is forbidden. The Prophet prohibited bowing or prostrating to humans. The lesson is profound: obedience to Allah must be unconditional; pride leads to spiritual ruin. The angelic obedience teaches believers to submit instantly to divine commands. Iblīs’s rebellion warns against letting perceived superiority—whether due to race, lineage, or material—block submission. The verse also establishes that jinn are a distinct creation, accountable to Allah. This part lays the groundwork for understanding the continuous spiritual war.
verse 50 | part 2 | Would you then take him and his descendants as patrons instead of Me, although they are your enemy? What an evil alternative for the wrongdoers to choose! | After recounting Iblīs’s disobedience, Allah poses a rhetorical rebuke: will humans take Satan and his offspring as protectors and allies, forsaking Allah? Despite Satan being a manifest enemy, many follow his whispers. The Prophet (peace be upon him) warned, “Satan flows through the human being like blood” (Sahih Bukhari). He also said, “There is none who has a protector (wali) except that he is with him, and Iblīs appoints his progeny to whisper to humans” (paraphrase). The term “his descendants” indicates Satan has offspring who continue his mission of misguidance. Taking them as patrons means obeying them in disbelief, sins, or turning away from divine guidance. The phrase “evil alternative” (bi’sa lil-ẓālimīna badalan) strongly condemns exchanging Allah’s guardianship for satanic allegiance. Historically, the pagan Arabs sought protection from jinn and idols, which were indirectly following Satan’s legacy. This verse calls them to abandon such false loyalties. The early Muslims, despite persecution, refused to compromise with the Quraysh’s satanic-inspired demands. Lesson: a believer must constantly examine their allegiances. Any choice that prioritizes the ego, worldly desires, or peer pressure over Allah’s commands is akin to taking Satan as a patron. True success lies in making Allah the sole Guardian. The verse awakens the reality of spiritual warfare, urging vigilance against the hidden enemy.
verse 51 | part 1 | I never called them to witness the creation of the heavens and the earth or even their own creation | Allah declares that He did not make Satan and his progeny witnesses to the creation of the universe or even their own creation. This exposes their lack of knowledge and authority. The false gods, idols, and devils have no insight into divine creation; they are themselves created and ignorant. The Prophet (peace be upon him) stressed that the unseen is exclusively Allah’s domain. The Quraysh associated partners with Allah who never participated in creation. Allah says elsewhere: “Do they associate with Him those who create nothing, but are themselves created?” (7:191). This verse is a logical argument against shirk. Those who are not even witnesses to creation cannot be creators, sustainers, or worthy of worship. The creation of the heavens and earth—the macrocosm—and the human self—the microcosm—are the greatest marvels; Allah alone orchestrated them without needing helpers or witnesses. Lesson: the worth of any object of worship is measured by its role in creation. Only the Creator deserves devotion. This demolishes the divinity claimed for angels, jinn, saints, or idols. It also highlights the intimate knowledge Allah has of every being; humans were created without their own consultation, emphasizing total dependency on Allah.
verse 51 | part 2 | nor would I take the misleaders as helpers | Continuing from the previous part, Allah states He would never take those who lead others astray (al-muḍillīn) as assistants. This includes Satan and his followers. Allah is Self-Sufficient (Al-Ghaniyy) and needs no aide. Associating partners or considering misleaders as divine helpers is an insult to Allah’s majesty. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Allah says: I am the most self-sufficient of partners; whoever does a deed in which he associates something else with Me, I abandon him and his shirk” (Sahih Muslim). The “misleaders” refer to devils and human leaders of falsehood. Historically, the Quraysh chiefs misled their people away from the Prophet. This verse assures that such misleaders have no share in divine authority. Even the prophets are servants, not helpers in creation. The lesson: all power and guidance belong to Allah. Seeking help through misguided individuals or entities is folly. True assistance comes only from Allah, and those who misguide will face punishment, not reward. It reinforces the oneness of Allah in His actions and Lordship.
verse 52 | part 1 | And beware of the Day He will say, “Call upon those you claimed were My associate-gods.” | Allah warns of the Day of Judgment when the disbelievers will be commanded to call upon their false gods for help. This is a scene of humiliation and exposure. The idols, angels, prophets, and jinn they worshipped will be summoned. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “On the Day of Resurrection, people will be gathered, and there will be an announcement: ‘Let every people follow what they used to worship.’ So whoever worshipped the sun will follow the sun, and whoever worshipped the moon will follow the moon, and whoever worshipped idols will follow idols…” (part of a long hadith in Sahih Bukhari). The false deities will not respond. The Arab pagans worshipped idols like Al-Lat, Al-‘Uzza, and Manat; this verse foretells their worthlessness on that Day. The call is ironic: the disbelievers relied on these associates in life, so now they are told to seek help from them. This is a severe chastisement before the actual punishment. Lesson: The objects of misplaced worship abandon their worshippers at the moment of greatest need. Only sincere devotion to Allah benefits on that Day. It is a powerful reminder to evaluate what or whom we ultimately rely upon.
verse 52 | part 2 | So they will call them, but will receive no response. And We will make them all share in the same doom. | In desperation, the disbelievers will call their gods, but there will be no answer. The false gods will disown them, as the Quran states elsewhere: “They will say, ‘Our Lord, these are the ones who led us astray; give them double punishment.’” (33:67-68). The phrase “We will make them all share in the same doom” (mawbiqā) indicates a barrier or a place of destruction—a shared punishment for both worshippers and the worshipped idols/devils. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The worshipper and the worshipped will be in the Hellfire, except those who were worshipped without their consent, like Jesus and Ezra” (paraphrase of a hadith context). The shared doom emphasizes that following falsehood implicates all parties, unless the worshipped were unwilling. The historical Meccan idols, along with their worshippers, face ruin. Lesson: Alliances in sin lead to collective punishment. No one will escape accountability by blaming their misleaders; both the follower and the misguiding leader share in the doom. This should deter people from blindly following traditions and call them to independent, sincere faith.
verse 53 | part 1 | The wicked will see the Fire and realize that they are bound to fall into it | The criminals (mujrimūn) will actually behold Hellfire, becoming certain that they are about to enter it. This is a moment of extreme terror. The verb “ẓannū” here means they will know with certainty, not conjecture. The Prophet (peace be upon him) described: “Hellfire will be brought on that Day, dragged by seventy thousand ropes, each rope pulled by seventy thousand angels” (Sahih Muslim). Seeing the Fire, the disbelievers will be overcome with dread, knowing there is no escape. Historically, the Quraysh denied the existence of Hell, calling it a myth. This verse confirms its reality. The psychological torment of anticipation is part of the punishment. Lesson: The certainty of judgment should motivate a person to live righteously. The vision of Hell is not a mere threat; it is a future reality that every wrongdoer will face. True belief involves accepting this unseen reality as if seen, shaping one’s actions to avoid that fate.
verse 53 | part 2 | and will find no way to avoid it | They will be unable to escape or turn away from the Fire. No refuge, no intercessor (except by Allah’s leave for the believers), no alternative path. The word “maṣrifā” means a place to turn or a way to deflect. In the worldly life, they could ignore warnings, but on that Day, there is no alternative. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “O people, save yourselves from the Fire, even by half a date (in charity)” (Sahih Bukhari). But after death, charity and escape are impossible. This highlights the finality of judgment. The lesson is urgent: the time to act is now, before death closes the door. One should seek Allah’s forgiveness while there is still opportunity, for when the Fire is seen, regret will not avail. It underscores the importance of taqwā—precaution against Hell through obedience.
verse 54 | part 1 | We have surely set forth in this Quran every kind of lesson for people | Allah declares that He has diversified the lessons, parables, and arguments in the Quran for humanity’s guidance. The word “ṣarrafnā” means to turn repeatedly, dispense in various forms: stories, commands, warnings, promises, and logical proofs. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The Quran contains the news of those before you, the judgment of what is among you, and the prophecies of what will come after you” (paraphrase from a narration). The Quraysh were given clear signs, yet many refused. The Quran offers examples from nature, history, and the unseen, covering every aspect needed for spiritual, moral, and social life. This comprehensiveness demonstrates divine mercy: no excuse remains for rejection. Lesson: The Quran is a complete guide; a believer should study it deeply to find the lesson for every situation. The diversity of its methods caters to different intellects and hearts, calling all to reflect and believe.
verse 54 | part 2 | but humankind is the most argumentative of all beings | Despite abundant guidance, humans are described as “the most argumentative” (akthara shay’in jadalan). The term “jadalan” refers to disputation, quarrelsomeness, and endless objections. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The most hated of men to Allah is the harshly argumentative” (Sahih Bukhari). The Quraysh persistently debated the Prophet, asking for physical miracles, splitting the moon, or altering the Quran. This trait is rooted in pride and the refusal to submit. Instead of accepting clear truth, many engage in verbal disputes to justify their desires. Lesson: The intellect is a tool for recognition of truth, not for endless debate. A believer must avoid fruitless argumentation and humbly accept divine wisdom. When truth is clear, arguing is a sign of disease. This verse counsels moderation in dialogue and prioritizing faith over ego.
verse 55 | part 1 | And nothing prevents people from believing when guidance comes to them and from seeking their Lord’s forgiveness | Allah identifies that there is no genuine obstacle preventing belief and repentance once guidance has arrived. The truth is clear; the only barriers are internal stubbornness and worldly attachment. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Allah extends His Hand at night to accept the repentance of the one who sinned during the day, and He extends His Hand during the day to accept the repentance of the one who sinned at night” (Sahih Muslim). The Quran and the Messenger provided sufficient proofs. The Quraysh had witnessed the Prophet’s character and the Quran’s miracle, yet they hesitated. The phrase “seeking their Lord’s forgiveness” connects belief with istighfār, showing that turning to Allah is always open. Lesson: Allah removes all excuses. People hinder themselves by their own choices. The path to forgiveness and faith is straightforward and always available. No one should delay.
verse 55 | part 2 | except their demand to meet the same fate of earlier deniers or that the torment would confront them face to face | The exception reveals the perverse conditions some set: they want the same punishment that destroyed previous nations—like the flood of Noah or the storm of ‘Ād—to come upon them before they believe. Or they want the torment to appear directly and tangibly. This demand is arrogance and mockery. The Prophet warned the Quraysh about divine punishment, and they taunted, “O Allah, if this is truly the truth from You, then rain down stones on us” (8:32). But such demanded punishment, when it comes, leaves no chance for repentance. The lesson: demanding spectacular signs before believing is a ruse of the ego. True faith accepts evidence without seeing catastrophic punishment. Procrastination and bargaining with God are destructive; one should embrace guidance immediately.
verse 56 | part 1 | We do not send the messengers except as deliverers of good news and warners. | The role of all messengers is clearly defined: bringers of glad tidings (bashīr) to the obedient and warners (nadhīr) to the disobedient. They are not sent to force belief or to bring arbitrary punishments on demand. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “I am only a warner, and death is the overtaker” (Sunan Ibn Majah). His duty was to convey, not to compel. The historical messengers, from Noah to Muhammad, shared this identical mission. This verse comforts the Prophet that his responsibility is to deliver the message clearly; he is not accountable for their rejection. The lesson: the prophetic method balances hope and fear. The callers to Islam today must emulate this: conveying mercy and warning without coercion. People’s final acceptance rests with Allah.
verse 56 | part 2 | But the disbelievers argue in falsehood, hoping to discredit the truth with it, and make a mockery of My revelations and warnings. | Instead of heeding the message, the disbelievers use false arguments (bāṭil) to refute the truth (ḥaqq). Their goal is to weaken the truth and ridicule divine signs. The Meccan chiefs, like Abu Jahl, mocked the Quran as ancient tales and asked for impossible proofs. The Prophet (peace be upon him) was deeply hurt by their mockery, but this verse explains it as a pattern of disbelievers across history. They used sophistry and ridicule, not sincere inquiry. The lesson: truth does not fear intellectual scrutiny, but mockery and false argumentation reveal a diseased heart. Believers should not engage in such debates that aim at triumph rather than truth. They must maintain dignity and not let mockery affect their faith, knowing Allah will preserve His truth.
verse 57 | part 1 | And who does more wrong than those who, when reminded of their Lord’s revelations, turn away from them and forget what their own hands have done? | This verse asks a rhetorical question, implying no one is more unjust than a person who, after being reminded of Allah’s clear signs, deliberately turns away and neglects the sins they committed. The reminder is meant to awaken, but they ignore it. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “All of my community will be forgiven except those who publicize their sins” (Sahih Bukhari), but forgetting one’s sins leads to impenitence. The phrase “forget what their own hands have done” means they do not reflect on their evil deeds or seek forgiveness. The Quraysh leaders heard the Quran and saw its truth but turned away due to pride. Lesson: The greatest wrong is to reject divine guidance after it becomes clear, and to remain heedless of one’s own misdeeds. Self-reflection and acceptance of correction are essential for spiritual growth.
verse 57 | part 2 | We have certainly cast veils over their hearts—leaving them unable to comprehend this Quran—and deafness in their ears. | Because of their persistent turning away and sin, Allah places a covering (akinnah) over their hearts and a heaviness (waqr) in their ears, so they neither understand nor hear the guidance. This is a divine consequence of their free choice. The Prophet (peace be upon him) supplicated for guidance for the Quraysh, but those who stubbornly rejected were sealed. It does not mean they have no choice initially; the veil comes after repeated rejection. The hadith says, “When a servant commits a sin, a black dot appears on his heart; if he repents, it is polished away. If he persists, the dot increases until it covers the heart” (Sunan At-Tirmidhi). Lesson: Sin hardens the heart and blocks spiritual perception. Continuous exposure to truth without acceptance can lead to a state of insensitivity. Therefore, immediate repentance and openness to guidance are crucial. It is a warning against procrastination.
verse 57 | part 3 | And if you O Prophet invite them to true guidance, they will never be rightly guided. | As a result of the sealing, even the Prophet’s best efforts cannot guide them. This does not negate free will in the initial stages but indicates that when the heart is sealed, guidance becomes impossible. Allah consoled the Prophet (peace be upon him) who desperately wanted his people to believe. He said, “You cannot guide whom you love, but Allah guides whom He wills” (28:56). The lesson: guidance is ultimately Allah’s gift. The caller should strive but never despair if some reject after truth is clear. It also instills humility: no human can force faith. One should pray for a receptive heart and avoid reaching the state of spiritual deadness.
verse 58 | part 1 | Your Lord is the All-Forgiving, Full of Mercy. | This verse opens with two beautiful names: Al-Ghafūr (the All-Forgiving) and Dhū ar-Raḥmah (Full of Mercy). Despite the warnings of punishment, Allah’s primary attribute with His servants is mercy and forgiveness. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Allah’s mercy outweighs His wrath” (Sahih Bukhari). He also said, “If you did not sin, Allah would wipe you out and bring a people who sin, then they seek forgiveness and He forgives them” (Sahih Muslim). The mention of mercy here gives hope that no matter how great the sin, repentance is accepted before death. Historical context: the Meccans who were being warned still had a chance to turn back. The lesson: despair of Allah’s mercy is a major sin. Believers should balance fear with hope, always returning to the forgiving Lord.
verse 58 | part 2 | If He were to seize them immediately for what they commit, He would have certainly hastened their punishment. But they have an appointed time, from which they will find no refuge. | Allah’s forbearance means He does not punish immediately for every sin, though He is able. If He were to take people to account right away, destruction would be swift. But He has set an appointed term (maw‘idā) — the Day of Judgment or a specific destruction — and no one can escape it. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Verily, Allah grants respite to the wrongdoer, but when He seizes him, He does not let him escape” (Sahih Bukhari). The early disbelievers were given time, but when punishment came, it was decisive. The lesson: do not be deceived by the delay of punishment; it is a test and a mercy, allowing time for repentance. The appointed time is certain; nobody can flee from it. This should spur immediate action toward righteousness.
verse 59 | part 1 | Those are the societies We destroyed when they persisted in wrong | Referring to past nations like ‘Ād, Thamūd, and the people of Lot, they were destroyed because they persisted in wrongdoing (ẓalamū). The Arabic “ahleknā” means complete annihilation. The Prophet (peace be upon him) passed by the ruins of Thamūd and warned his companions not to enter except weeping, as a reminder of punishment. The persistence in sin, despite warnings from their prophets, led to their downfall. The lesson: history is filled with examples of destroyed civilizations. Those who refuse to learn from the past are doomed to repeat it. Righteousness is the only safeguard.
verse 59 | part 2 | and We had set a time for their destruction | The destruction was not arbitrary; a fixed term (maw‘idā) was decreed. This shows divine predestination and justice. Each nation had its appointed lifespan until their transgression reached a limit. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “No nation is destroyed until it is presented with clear evidence” (paraphrase). The appointed time also gave them a chance to repent. The lesson: Allah’s decree is precise; no nation perishes before its time. This provides comfort to the oppressed that justice is scheduled, and warns oppressors that their deadline is known only to Allah.
verse 60 | part 1 | And remember when Moses said to his young assistant | This begins the story of Musa (Moses) and his journey to find Al-Khaḍir. Moses addresses his young servant (fatā), identified in hadith as Yūsha‘ ibn Nūn (Joshua). The Prophet (peace be upon him) explained that Moses was once asked, “Who is the most knowledgeable of people?” He answered, “I am,” so Allah revealed to him that there was a servant at the junction of the two seas who had more knowledge (Sahih Bukhari). So Moses set out with his assistant. The mention of “young assistant” shows the importance of companionship in seeking knowledge. The lesson: even a great prophet like Moses humbly sought learning from another, teaching humility in the pursuit of wisdom. No one is self-sufficient in knowledge.
verse 60 | part 2 | “I will never give up until I reach the junction of the two seas, even if I travel for ages.” | Moses’s determination is remarkable. “Junction of the two seas” (majma‘ al-baḥrayn) is where the two bodies of water meet, traditionally thought to be where the Gulf of Suez meets the Red Sea or some other location. He was prepared to travel for a long period (ḥuqubā) — meaning years or ages — to find the knowledgeable servant. This shows the value of seeking knowledge: one must be ready for sacrifice and effort. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Whoever treads a path in search of knowledge, Allah will make easy for him a path to Paradise” (Sahih Muslim). Moses exemplifies this. Lesson: determination in the path of Allah yields immense reward. Spiritual journeys require patience and perseverance, regardless of the distance or time.
verse 61 | part 1 | But when they finally reached the point where the seas met, they forgot their salted fish | Upon reaching the rock at the junction, Moses and his servant forgot their packed fish, which was their provision. The fish was dead and salted, but Allah revived it and it slipped into the water. The forgetting was part of the divine plan to mark the meeting spot. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The fish was not forgotten except that it was caused to be forgotten as a sign for Moses to meet Al-Khaḍir” (as per the hadith in Bukhari). The word “nasiyā” (they forgot) indicates human error, which can be divinely guided to a greater purpose. Lesson: sometimes what appears as a mistake or forgetfulness in life is actually part of Allah’s plan leading to a greater good. Patience and attentiveness to signs are required.
verse 61 | part 2 | and it made its way into the sea, slipping away wondrously | The fish miraculously came to life and took its path into the sea through a burrow or tunnel (sarabā). The word “‘ajabā” means amazingly or wondrously. This miraculous event was the sign they missed initially. In the hadith, the Prophet described that the water became like an arch over the fish’s path, and that path was preserved. This sign indicated the exact meeting point. Allah’s power to revive the dead fish is a sign of resurrection. The lesson: miracles occur by Allah’s will, even in daily journeys, but one must have a perceptive heart to notice them. The incident teaches that what seems impossible (a dead, salted fish swimming away) is easy for Allah.
verse 62 | part 1 | When they had passed further, he said to his assistant, “Bring us our meal! We have certainly been exhausted by today’s journey.” | After traveling beyond the junction, Moses felt hunger and fatigue, so he asked for the meal. The journey had been long and tiring. This shows the human side of even a mighty prophet: he needed food and rest. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “I am only a human being like you; I forget as you forget, so if I forget, remind me” (Sahih Bukhari). The request for food triggered the recollection of the forgotten fish. Lesson: Even the most spiritual people have physical needs; Islam does not expect superhuman endurance. The request also set the stage for realizing the loss, teaching that ordinary needs can lead to extraordinary spiritual discoveries.
verse 62 | part 2 | “Bring us our meal! We have certainly been exhausted by today’s journey.” | The exhaustion (laqad laqīnā min safarinā hādhā naṣabā) emphasizes the difficulty of the quest. Seeking knowledge and divine wisdom often involves physical and mental strain. The Companions of the Prophet endured similar exhaustion in battles and travels for the sake of Islam. The reward is commensurate with the effort. The lesson: no valuable attainment comes without fatigue. The journey to Allah and knowledge requires sacrifice. This verse encourages believers not to give up when tired, but to push forward, trusting that the goal is near.
verse 63 | part 1 | He replied, “Do you remember when we rested by the rock? That is when I forgot the fish.” | The assistant recalls the exact spot: the rock where they had rested. He admits forgetfulness. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “If anyone forgets something, he should remember Allah” (paraphrase). The rock became the landmark of the appointed meeting with Al-Khaḍir. This shows that what seemed a lapse was actually the marker. The servant’s honesty in admitting his mistake is noteworthy. Lesson: admitting forgetfulness or error is a virtue, not a weakness. It allowed Moses to trace back the steps. In spiritual journeys, one must be honest about oversights, as they may be the key to the next stage. Also, places associated with divine signs become blessed.
verse 63 | part 2 | None made me forget to mention this except Satan. And the fish made its way into the sea miraculously. | The servant attributes the forgetfulness to Satan’s whisper, acknowledging that forgetfulness in matters of obedience can be from Satan. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Satan ties three knots at the back of your head when you sleep… and if you wake up and remember Allah, one knot is undone” (Sahih Bukhari). The servant didn’t blame the fish but himself. He also confirms the fish’s miraculous escape. This is a moment of realization: the sign was missed due to satanic distraction. Lesson: Satan seeks to cause forgetfulness in spiritual tasks. Seeking refuge in Allah and constant remembrance protects against such lapses. When one realizes a missed sign, they should retrace their steps as Moses did.
verse 64 | part 1 | Moses responded, “That is exactly what we were looking for.” | Moses recognizes that the loss of the fish and the rock location is the exact sign he was seeking to find Al-Khaḍir. Instead of anger, he rejoices. This shows his spiritual insight. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Wondrous is the affair of the believer, for there is good for him in everything” (Sahih Muslim). What seemed a negative event (forgetting food) was actually the key. Lesson: believers look for divine signs in apparent setbacks. The “lost” fish was the compass pointing to their destination. This fosters optimism and trust in Allah’s plan even in minor mishaps.
verse 64 | part 2 | So they returned, retracing their footsteps. | Without hesitation, Moses and his servant went back the way they came, following their tracks (qaṣaṣā). Retracing footsteps indicates diligence and the willingness to correct course. They did not consider it a wasted journey; rather, the return was the final leg of the quest. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “And whoever returns to something, it is as if he never left it” (in some contexts). The lesson: when one realizes a mistake, the right action is to correct it immediately, no matter the effort. Humility in retracing steps to regain the right path is a sign of sincere seekers.
verse 65 | part 1 | There they found a servant of Ours | At the rock, they found the promised servant, Al-Khaḍir. The Quran does not mention his name explicitly; he is called “a servant from among Our servants.” Most scholars identify him as Al-Khaḍir, a prophet or a righteous man granted special knowledge. The Prophet (peace be upon him) confirmed his identity in Sahih Bukhari. That he is a “servant” (abd) is the highest honor. Allah says “Ours,” indicating closeness and chosenness. This meeting was a direct answer to Moses’s long journey. Lesson: the true scholars and guides are those whom Allah honors with servitude and special knowledge. The search for a guide should be undertaken with the same zeal as Moses showed, and when found, they should be approached with respect.
verse 65 | part 2 | to whom We had granted mercy from Us and enlightened with knowledge of Our Own. | Al-Khaḍir was given raḥmah (mercy), meaning prophethood or divine inspiration and protection. He was also given ‘ilm ladunnī (knowledge from Allah’s own presence), which is intuitive, esoteric knowledge not bound by apparent laws. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Al-Khaḍir was given knowledge that Moses did not have, and Moses was given knowledge that Al-Khaḍir did not have” (Sahih Bukhari). This shows that Allah distributes knowledge variously; no single person encompasses all. The mercy and knowledge are a direct gift, not academic learning. Lesson: true beneficial knowledge is a divine gift, acquired through piety and Allah’s mercy. Believers should seek both revealed and inspired knowledge, while remaining humble, as even a great prophet like Moses had much to learn from a servant not generally known.
verse 66 | part 1 | Moses said to him, “May I follow you, provided that you teach me some of the right guidance you have been taught?” | Moses approaches with extreme politeness: he asks permission (hal attabi‘uka) and frames it as a conditional request, showing deference. He wants to learn from the right guidance (rushdā) Al-Khaḍir was taught. Despite being a major prophet who spoke with Allah, Moses shows no pride. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Humility raises a person’s status” (Sahih Muslim). This exemplifies the manners of the seeker of knowledge: ask permission, express the desire to learn, and acknowledge that the teacher has something special. Lesson: Knowledge cannot be taken by force; it requires humility, request, and respect for the teacher. No matter one’s own status, there is always someone to learn from. The ultimate goal is guidance, not boasting.
verse 67 | part 1 | He said, “You certainly cannot be patient enough with me.” | Al-Khaḍir responds with a prediction: Moses will not have the patience to accompany him because he will see actions that seem wrong outwardly. This is a test. The knowledge Al-Khaḍir has is of hidden realities, while Moses has the apparent law. The Prophet (peace be upon him) explained that Moses was eager but struggled with what he saw. The statement is not a rejection but a preparation. Lesson: Different levels of knowledge can cause conflict if one lacks patience. A student must trust the teacher’s wisdom, especially when things appear contradictory to one’s understanding. Rushing to judgment is a common failure.
verse 68 | part 1 | “And how can you be patient with what is beyond your realm of knowledge?” | Al-Khaḍir explains why: Moses cannot be patient with matters he does not fully comprehend. The phrase “lam tuḥiṭ bihi khubrā” means not encompassing knowledge. Human patience breaks when understanding is absent. The lesson: many trials in life, and even in spiritual training, involve wisdom not immediately apparent. Patience (ṣabr) is tied to trust. Believers must accept that Allah’s wisdom may exceed their comprehension, just as Moses had to accept Al-Khaḍir’s actions.
verse 69 | part 1 | Moses assured him, “You will find me patient, Allah willing” | Moses promises patience, but he immediately qualifies it with “Allah willing” (in shā’ Allāh). This shows he learned from the earlier command (verse 23-24) to always condition future actions on Allah’s will. The Prophet (peace be upon him) praised Moses for this, as it showed his reliance on Allah. The promise indicates his sincere intention, but he acknowledges that true patience comes from Allah. Lesson: even with firm resolve, one must depend on Allah for the ability to fulfill promises. The phrase “in shā’ Allāh” is essential in all plans, acknowledging human weakness.
verse 69 | part 2 | “and I will not disobey any of your orders.” | Moses further promises obedience: he will not violate any command. This was his commitment to the path of learning. Al-Khaḍir’s subsequent condition is the test of this promise. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Hearing and obeying is the duty of a Muslim, in what he loves and hates, as long as he is not commanded to sin” (Sahih Bukhari). Moses’s statement reflects the proper student attitude: total compliance. Lesson: The seeker of spiritual knowledge must be willing to obey the guide’s instructions, even if they seem unusual, provided they do not contradict clear divine law. Obedience is the door to understanding.
verse 70 | part 1 | He responded, “Then if you follow me, do not question me about anything until I myself clarify it for you.” | Al-Khaḍir accepts with a strict condition: no questions or objections until he himself explains. This is the pact. The “anything” (shay’) includes any action he performs. This is a test of patience and trust. The Prophet (peace be upon him) used to train his companions similarly sometimes, asking them to accept matters of faith before questioning. This condition was crucial because the knowledge Al-Khaḍir possessed was of a hidden nature that would provoke immediate objection. Lesson: Deep learning sometimes requires suspension of immediate judgment. The student must observe and wait for the teacher’s clarification, trusting that the explanation will come. This is difficult but essential for mastering wisdom beyond surface appearances.
verse 71 | part 1 | So they set out, but after they had boarded a ship, the man made a hole in it. | They embarked on a ship, and Al-Khaḍir damaged it by making a hole (kharaqahā), an act that appears destructive and dangerous. To Moses, this was an unprovoked act of vandalism endangering lives. The ship likely belonged to poor people. This was the first of three puzzling actions. The lesson: the first test of patience involved property damage that seemed unjust. This foreshadows that divine intervention in the world may sometimes appear harmful but holds hidden mercy.
verse 71 | part 2 | Moses protested, “Have you done this to drown its people? You have certainly done a terrible thing!” | Moses immediately objects, forgetting his promise. He exclaims that the hole could drown the passengers, calling it a terrible deed (imrā). The word “imrā” denotes a grievous, evil affair. This shows his human reaction to apparent injustice. The Prophet (peace be upon him) related that Moses said this out of his deep sense of justice given by the Law. His objection is based on the outward ruling. The lesson: first impressions can lead even a prophet to break a pact. It’s a gentle reminder of the difficulty of withholding judgment. Yet, his moral outrage was not wrong in itself; the timing of the question was the issue.
verse 72 | part 1 | He replied, “Did I not say that you cannot have patience with me?” | Al-Khaḍir gently reminds Moses of their agreement, pointing out his lack of patience. This is not an angry rebuke but a teaching moment, reinforcing the condition. The reminder is necessary to help Moses realize his haste. The lesson: a good teacher corrects with wisdom and patience, not harshness. The student needs to be reminded of their commitment when they err. For Moses, this was humbling; the great prophet was learning the discipline of silence and trust.
verse 73 | part 1 | Moses pleaded, “Excuse me for forgetting, and do not be hard on me.” | Moses immediately acknowledges his mistake: he forgot the condition. He asks for pardon (lā tu’ākhidhnī) and pleads that Al-Khaḍir not make the journey excessively difficult for him (lā turhiqnī min amrī ‘usrā). The word “turhiqnī” means to burden or overburden. Moses’s admission of forgetfulness is honest; he did not intentionally break the pact. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “My community is forgiven for their mistakes, forgetfulness, and what they are compelled to do” (Sunan Ibn Majah). The lesson: When one slips, immediate admission and asking for leniency is the right response. The path of learning is full of stumbles; the teacher’s compassion is vital. This interaction deepened Moses’s humility and prepared him for the remaining tests.
verse 74 | part 1 | So they proceeded until they came across a boy, and the man killed him. | After the ship incident, Moses and Al-Khaḍir continued their journey. They encountered a young boy (ghulām), and Al-Khaḍir killed him. This act, seen outwardly, is even more shocking than the hole in the ship—it is the taking of an innocent life. According to authentic hadith in Sahih Bukhari, the boy was playing with other children, and Al-Khaḍir deliberately killed him. The Prophet (peace be upon him) explained that this boy was destined to grow up to be a disbeliever who would oppress his parents and lead them into disbelief and tyranny. Al-Khaḍir acted upon hidden knowledge given by Allah, not on his own whim. The death of the boy was, in reality, a mercy for the parents and society, preventing future grave sins. The outward horror contradicts the inner divine wisdom. Moses, bound by the law of retaliation and the sanctity of life, could not comprehend this. This incident tests the limits of human patience and trust in divine decree. The lesson is profound: sometimes what appears as a senseless tragedy contains a hidden blessing that only Allah knows. Believers are called to accept Allah’s qadar (predestination) with patience, trusting that the All-Wise does nothing in vain. It also shows that some knowledge—like the fate of souls—is exclusively with Allah, and His actions, even when seemingly harsh, stem from ultimate mercy. The incident prepares the heart for understanding that worldly loss may be eternal gain.
verse 74 | part 2 | Moses protested, “Have you killed an innocent soul, who killed no one? You have certainly done a horrible thing.” | Moses, unable to contain his shock, immediately protested despite his previous promise of silence. He invoked the Mosaic Law which forbids killing a soul unless in retribution for murder or corruption. The phrase “nafsan zakiyyah” means a pure, innocent soul that had not committed any crime. To Moses, this was a clear violation of divine command. He used the word “nukrā” (horrible thing), even stronger than “imrā” (terrible thing) used for the ship incident. This shows his deep moral outrage. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “No person is killed unjustly but that a share of the guilt is upon the first son of Adam, because he was the first to institute killing” (Sahih Bukhari). Moses’s reaction was based on the apparent reality, which is the basis of human judgment. However, he had again broken the condition of not asking questions. His sincerity and sense of justice are not blameworthy; the lesson lies in the timing and the nature of hidden knowledge. It teaches that even prophets may find certain divine decrees difficult to accept without full knowledge. The believer must learn to suspend immediate judgment and trust that Allah’s actions, or those He permits, have a greater wisdom. It also illustrates the importance of the law: outwardly, killing is abhorrent, and Islam upholds the sanctity of life. The hidden exception here is a rare divine intervention, not a norm for human conduct.
verse 75 | part 1 | He answered, “Did I not tell you that you cannot have patience with me?” | Al-Khaḍir’s response is a calm and patient reminder of his earlier condition. He does not rebuke Moses harshly but points to his own earlier statement. This is a teaching moment: patience is not merely about enduring hardship but about withholding questions when one lacks full knowledge. The repetition of “I told you” emphasizes that Moses was forewarned, yet his human nature overcame his resolve. In a hadith, the Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The strong man is not the one who can overpower others; rather, the strong man is the one who controls himself when angry” (Sahih Bukhari). Moses’s impatience stemmed from righteous anger, but the spiritual station required a higher degree of self-restraint. The lesson for seekers of knowledge is to develop the discipline of silence, observation, and trust in the teacher’s wisdom, as long as the teacher is trustworthy and guided. Al-Khaḍir’s reminder also shows that learning often requires repeated correction, and a good teacher does not abandon the student after the first slip. This patience on the teacher’s part is also a divine trait, reflecting Allah’s forbearance with His servants.
verse 76 | part 1 | Moses replied, “If I ever question you about anything after this, then do not keep me in your company, for by then I would have given you enough of an excuse.” | Moses acknowledges his fault and sets a firm condition for himself: if he objects again, Al-Khaḍir has the right to part ways. The phrase “qad balaghta min ladunnī ‘udhrā” means “you have reached an excuse from me” – i.e., I would have given you sufficient reason to leave me. This shows Moses’s humility and self-awareness. He does not try to justify his lapses but accepts responsibility and imposes a penalty on himself. The Prophet (peace be upon him) praised self-accountability: “The wise person is the one who takes himself to account and works for what is after death” (Sunan At-Tirmidhi). This third chance was a mercy, but Moses bound himself strictly. The lesson: a sincere student admits mistakes, sets boundaries for improvement, and accepts the consequences of failure. It also illustrates that human resolve is weak without divine assistance; Moses had said “Allah willing” earlier but still slipped. Reliance on Allah must be continuous. This attitude earned him the final leg of the journey, leading to the revelation of hidden wisdom. In life, when we falter, we should not despair but recommit with stronger determination, while remaining humble about our limitations.
verse 77 | part 1 | So they moved on until they came to the people of a town. They asked them for food, but the people refused to give them hospitality. There they found a wall ready to collapse, so the man set it right. | The duo reached a town whose inhabitants were miserly and inhospitable, refusing even basic food to travelers. In Arabian culture, refusal of hospitality was a grave social sin. Despite this cold treatment, they came across a wall (jidār) that was about to fall (yanqaḍḍu). Al-Khaḍir, without asking for any payment, repaired it and made it straight (aqāmahu). This act of unrewarded kindness seemed illogical to Moses: why help people who had just mistreated them? The hadith in Sahih Bukhari clarifies that they asked for food as guests, a right that should have been honored. The refusal indicated the town’s moral corruption. Yet Al-Khaḍir’s action showed that divine mercy may extend to the undeserving for the sake of hidden righteousness (the orphan boys). The wall repair was a physical effort requiring strength and skill, demonstrating that even a knowledgeable saint works with his hands for a greater good. The lesson: a believer should not withhold good deeds even from those who have wronged them, if there is a higher wisdom. Charity and service are not dependent on people’s gratitude but on Allah’s pleasure. Also, the story foreshadows the hidden treasure and the righteous father, showing that a person’s piety can benefit their descendants in unexpected ways.
verse 77 | part 2 | Moses protested, “If you wanted, you could have demanded a fee for this.” | Moses saw an opportunity for just compensation, especially given their need and the townspeople’s inhospitality. By repairing the wall, Al-Khaḍir had provided a service; charging a wage would have been lawful and practically helpful. Moses’s suggestion was based on common sense and even the prophetic tradition that a worker deserves payment. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Give the worker his wages before his sweat dries” (Sunan Ibn Majah). However, Moses had once again breached the agreement by offering unsolicited advice, revealing his impatience. This was the third and final infraction. The word “ajrā” (fee) indicates a material compensation, which seemed reasonable. Yet Al-Khaḍir’s action was not transactional; it was a divinely commanded act of preservation. Moses’s focus on immediate worldly benefit contrasted with the hidden long-term good. The lesson: sometimes spiritually guided actions appear economically irrational; faith requires trusting that Allah’s reward is better than immediate worldly gain. The incident teaches that not every good deed should be commercialized; some acts are for Allah alone, whose reward is infinite. It also highlights the gap between human cost-benefit analysis and divine wisdom.
verse 78 | part 1 | He replied, “This is the parting of our ways. I will explain to you what you could not bear patiently.” | With the third infraction, Al-Khaḍir announces the end of their companionship as per the condition Moses himself had set. The phrase “hādhā firāqu baynī wa baynika” is a dignified separation. Importantly, Al-Khaḍir does not part in anger but promises a full explanation (ta’wīl) of the three actions that Moses could not bear patiently. This demonstrates the perfect pedagogy: the student first observes, then is given the interpretation. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Convey from me even if it is one verse” (Sahih Bukhari), stressing the importance of explanation. The parting underscores that some levels of knowledge can only be attained after a test of patience; failure to meet the test ends the opportunity. Yet mercy is shown by providing the reasons, so Moses is not left in confusion. The lesson: in the journey of learning, there are milestones where the teacher and student must part if the student’s capacity is exceeded. However, a true teacher provides closure. This also reflects that companionship with the righteous is a trust; violating its conditions can lead to loss of that blessing. It teaches believers to hold fast to the etiquettes of discipleship, particularly not rushing to judge divine or scholarly actions before grasping their inner meanings.
verse 79 | part 1 | “As for the ship, it belonged to some poor people, working at sea. So I intended to damage it, for there was a tyrant king ahead of them who seizes every good ship by force.” | Al-Khaḍir explains the first incident. The ship’s owners were poor, honest workers (masākīn) who depended on it for their livelihood. A tyrannical king was seizing all functional ships by force. By making a hole that rendered the ship defective, Al-Khaḍir ensured the king would reject it, thus saving the owners from complete loss. The damage was superficial and repairable, whereas seizure would have been permanent. The word “a‘ībahā” (I intended to damage it) shows it was a deliberate act with a merciful purpose. This illustrates the permissibility of choosing the lesser of two evils to prevent a greater harm, a well-known Islamic juridical principle. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “No harm shall be inflicted or reciprocated” (Sunan Ibn Majah), but jurists allow minor harm to avert major harm. The hidden mercy here was that the poor families’ source of income was preserved. Moses saw destruction; the reality was protection. The lesson: believers must understand that Allah’s decree may involve apparent setbacks that save them from greater calamities. What appears as a misfortune might be a divine shield. This teaches trust in Allah’s plan when worldly logic fails and the acceptance that Allah’s wisdom works through causes beyond our sight.
verse 80 | part 1 | “And as for the boy, his parents were true believers, and we feared that he would pressure them into defiance and disbelief.” | The second explanation: the killed boy had believing parents, but Allah knew that if he lived, he would oppress them and drag them into tyranny (ṭughyān) and disbelief (kufr). The word “khashīnā” (we feared) refers to Allah’s knowledge that this would certainly happen, and it was a protective measure, not an emotional fear. The parent’s faith was precious, and the boy’s future corruption would destroy their spiritual well-being. In a hadith, the Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Allah has decreed for the son of Adam his share of adultery… and he will inevitably commit it” (Sahih Bukhari), indicating that some are destined for certain sins. This divine intervention cut off a greater evil. The killing of a child before puberty who is destined to be a disbeliever is within Allah’s absolute sovereignty, but it is not a rule for humans to apply. The lesson: a believer’s environment and offspring are tests. Sometimes Allah removes something apparently precious to protect one’s faith. The parents’ loss was severe, but Allah compensated them with a better child (verse 81). This highlights the principle that faith is the greatest wealth; preserving it may require painful losses, and Allah’s compensation is always superior. It also teaches about the unseen knowledge of Allah regarding outcomes, urging parents to pray for righteous offspring and trust in divine choices.
verse 81 | part 1 | “So we hoped that their Lord would give them another, more virtuous and caring in his place.” | The intended outcome: Allah would replace the boy with a child who was better in purity (zakāh) and more merciful (ruḥmā) towards his parents. The “we hoped” (aradnā) indicates the divine will for good. The replacement was both spiritual and emotional: “zakāh” implies righteousness and piety, while “ruḥmā” implies filial tenderness. This came true; the parents were blessed with a daughter or son who was a source of comfort and piety. In Sahih Bukhari, the hadith mentions that they were granted a daughter who later gave birth to a prophet (some narrations say she was the mother of a prophet). This shows Allah’s compensation far exceeded the loss. The lesson: earthly loss for Allah’s sake is never vain; it is an investment for a better return. The parents’ patience would be rewarded immensely. It also encourages believers to accept Allah’s decrees in children—whether death or disability—knowing that Allah may give better or elevate their ranks in Paradise. The attributes of the replacement child show the ideal Muslim offspring: pure in faith and kind to parents. This sets a standard for what parents should pray for.
verse 82 | part 1 | “And as for the wall, it belonged to two orphan boys in the city, and under the wall was a treasure that belonged to them, and their father had been a righteous man.” | The third act was to repair a wall covering a treasure (kanz) belonging to two orphan boys. Their father was a righteous man (ṣāliḥan), and due to his piety, Allah protected his orphans’ inheritance. The treasure could be gold, knowledge, or a valuable object. If the wall had collapsed, the treasure would have been exposed and stolen by the corrupt townspeople. Al-Khaḍir rebuilt it, preserving the wealth until the boys reached maturity (ashuddahumā) and could claim it. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The best thing to bequeath is a righteous child” (paraphrase), and here the father’s righteousness benefited his children after his death. This shows that good deeds and piety have intergenerational blessings. The lesson: parents should focus on their own righteousness, as it will protect their offspring in ways unseen. It also underscores the divine care for orphans; Islam repeatedly commands the protection of orphan’s property. The hidden treasure symbolizes the fruits of faith that are preserved by Allah for the righteous. The act of Al-Khaḍir, though unpaid and seemingly illogical, was an act of pure charity for the sake of Allah, honoring a righteous man’s legacy.
verse 82 | part 2 | “So your Lord willed that these children should come of age and retrieve their treasure, as a mercy from your Lord. I did not do it all on my own. This is the explanation of what you could not bear patiently.” | Al-Khaḍir concludes that all actions were carried out by Allah’s will and mercy, not his own volition. He emphasizes “raḥmatan min rabbik” (a mercy from your Lord) to show the overarching kindness behind the seemingly harsh acts. The statement “I did not do it on my own” (mā fa‘altuhu ‘an amrī) clarifies that he was an instrument of divine command, possessing no independent power. This reinforces pure monotheism: even the saints and prophets act by Allah’s permission. The phrase “that is the interpretation (ta’wīl) of what you could not bear patiently” wraps the story, confirming that patience (ṣabr) is the key to unlocking the deeper meanings of life’s trials. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “How wondrous is the affair of the believer! All his affairs are good for him… if something good happens, he is grateful; if something bad happens, he is patient, and that is good for him” (Sahih Muslim). The lesson: every trial has a hidden wisdom that may be revealed later, or it may remain secret, but Allah’s mercy is always the foundation. Believers must cultivate patience, trusting that Allah’s plan is ultimately benevolent. The story of Moses and Al-Khaḍir thus becomes a timeless manual for dealing with unexplained suffering and divine decree.
verse 83 | part 1 | They ask you O Prophet about Dhul-Qarnain. Say, “I will relate to you something of his narrative.” | The surah shifts to the fourth story. The Quraysh, prompted by the Jews of Madinah, asked the Prophet (peace be upon him) about Dhul-Qarnain (the Two-Horned One) as a test of his prophethood. “Dhul-Qarnain” means “possessor of two horns” — interpretations range from a great king who reached the east and west (like Cyrus the Great), to a literal reference to his two braids or helmet horns, or two ages he ruled. The Prophet was commanded to respond with “I will recite to you a remembrance” (sa-atlū ‘alaykum minhu dhikrā), indicating that the Quranic account is sufficient and true, without need for speculation. The Quran does not name him, focusing on his qualities and deeds. The Jews had detailed accounts in their traditions (possibly about Alexander the Great as a monotheist king, though historical Alexander was a polytheist; some say Cyrus). This question was a trap: if the Prophet gave a wrong answer, they would discredit him. But Allah provided the exact narrative. Lesson: the Quran offers what is beneficial, not excessive historical details. Believers should be content with the divine version, as it contains moral guidance. The story illustrates just leadership, reliance on Allah, and the use of power for good.
verse 84 | part 1 | Surely We established him in the land, and gave him the means to all things. | Allah granted Dhul-Qarnain firm authority (makkannā lahu fī al-arḍ) and the “means” (asbāb) to achieve everything. These asbāb refer to knowledge, power, resources, and the tools to travel vast distances and govern effectively. This shows that his power was not self-derived but a gift from Allah. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Authority is a trust; it will be a disgrace and regret on the Day of Resurrection except for one who takes it with right and fulfills its duties” (Sahih Muslim). Dhul-Qarnain used his power righteously, spreading justice and monotheism. The phrase “min kulli shay’in sababā” indicates that Allah gave him the path and methodology to accomplish his goals, whether in military campaigns, diplomacy, or exploration. The historical context suggests a world ruler who traveled to the sunset and sunrise. The lesson: leadership and ability are divine trusts. One who is given power must recognize it as from Allah, use it for justice, and not become arrogant. The “means” also symbolize that worldly success requires taking the right means, but ultimate facilitation is from Allah. Believers should seek the means while trusting the One who grants them.
verse 85 | part 1 | So he travelled a course. | Dhul-Qarnain embarked on a journey westward, following a path (sababā) — the same word used for “means” — indicating he utilized the resources given to him. The journey was not aimless; he followed a specific route of conquest or discovery. This action shows proactive use of divine gifts: he didn’t sit idle but explored and expanded his rule. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Take advantage of five before five: your youth before your old age, your health before your sickness, your wealth before your poverty, your free time before your preoccupation, and your life before your death” (Mustadrak al-Hakim). Dhul-Qarnain utilized his power and time. The lesson: believers must actively strive in the earth, using their God-given abilities for righteous purposes. Traveling and learning about different peoples is encouraged to establish justice and spread truth.
verse 86 | part 1 | until he reached the setting point of the sun, which appeared to him to be setting in a spring of murky water, where he found some people. | He reached the far west, where the sun appeared to set in a dark, muddy spring (‘ayn ḥami’ah). This is a description from the observer’s perspective: as he stood on the shore, the sun seemed to sink into the water at the horizon. It is not a literal statement that the sun enters the earth. The Quran speaks in human perceptual terms. The “murky water” indicates a large body of water—ocean or sea—with dark, muddy shores. The Prophet (peace be upon him) confirmed that the sun prostrates under the Throne each night (Sahih Bukhari), but that is a metaphysical prostration, not a physical descent. The people he found there were likely disbelieving or simple folk. This journey shows Allah’s power over the earth’s extremes and the diversity of creation. Lesson: the Quran uses phenomenological language; when we see the sun set, we can reflect on the Creator’s majesty. Dhul-Qarnain’s reach to the edges of the known world symbolizes the extent a righteous ruler can spread justice. It also teaches that encountering new peoples is a test of how to deal with them justly.
verse 86 | part 2 | We said, “O Dhul-Qarnain! Either punish them or treat them kindly.” | Allah gave him a choice regarding the people he found: either punish them for their misdeeds or adopt a path of goodness (ḥusnā) towards them. This divine instruction indicates that Dhul-Qarnain was a prophet or a divinely guided king who received revelation or inspiration. The choice reflects the principle of free will and justice: a ruler has the discretion to punish or forgive based on the situation. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Forgive when you are in a position of power” (paraphrase, similar in spirit to many hadith). It shows that leadership involves moral decisions, and mercy is preferred when possible. The lesson: authority must be exercised with wisdom; sometimes punishment is necessary to maintain justice, but kindness and rehabilitation are superior. Islamic governance principles emphasize giving people a chance to reform before punishment. This also prefigures the Prophet’s own approach: after the conquest of Makkah, he forgave his former persecutors.
verse 86 | part 3 | He responded, “Whoever does wrong will be punished by us, then will be returned to their Lord, Who will punish them with a horrible torment.” | Dhul-Qarnain announced his policy: those who persist in wrongdoing (ẓulm)—shirk or oppression—will face worldly punishment from him. But beyond that, they will be returned to Allah for an even more severe eternal punishment (nukrā). This shows he combined worldly justice with eschatological warning. He did not claim that his punishment was the final end; he pointed to the Hereafter. This aligns with the prophetic mission. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “When a judge judges, he must strive to be just” (Sunan Abu Dawud). The lesson: a Muslim ruler must establish law and order, but must also remind people of the ultimate accountability before Allah. Worldly punishment is limited; true justice is served in the afterlife. This instills both fear and responsibility in subjects, and keeps the ruler from arrogance, knowing Allah is the final Judge.
verse 88 | part 1 | “As for those who believe and do good, they will have the finest reward, and we will assign them easy commands.” | For the believers and righteous, Dhul-Qarnain promises the best reward (ḥusnā) — Paradise in the Hereafter, and in this world he would speak to them with gentle, easy commands (qawlan yusrā) — not burdening them beyond capacity. This echoes the Quranic principle: “Allah does not burden a soul beyond its capacity” (2:286). It also reflects the Prophetic method: ease and facilitation in religious and worldly matters. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Make things easy, and do not make them difficult; give glad tidings, and do not repel people” (Sahih Bukhari). Dhul-Qarnain’s justice combined firmness against evil with gentleness towards the good. This is a model of leadership: encourage virtue and faith, while being compassionate and practical. Lesson: those in authority should reward goodness and create a supportive environment for the righteous. The “easy commands” indicate that religious obligations should be applied with wisdom and mercy, not harshness. This promotes a healthy society where people are motivated to believe and do good.
verse 89 | part 1 | Then he travelled a different course. | After completing his western mission, Dhul-Qarnain took another path (sababā) towards the east, utilizing the means Allah provided. This shows relentless effort and ambition for good. He did not rest on his laurels but continued his journey to spread justice and knowledge. The Prophet (peace be upon him) was similarly tireless in his mission. The verse implies that a believer’s life is a series of purposeful journeys, always seeking to fulfill Allah’s will. Lesson: after achieving one goal, a righteous person sets out for the next, never succumbing to complacency. The path of the east is the path of light, symbolizing the spread of guidance to new horizons.
verse 90 | part 1 | until he reached the rising point of the sun. He found it rising on a people for whom We had provided no shelter from it. | Dhul-Qarnain arrived at the far east where the sun rose upon a people who had no covering (sitr) — no buildings, trees, or clothing to shield them from the sun. They might have lived in a primitive, open land near the equator, or as some scholars suggest, they were barely clothed and lacked civilization. The Quran uses this vivid description to illustrate the diversity of human conditions and the mercy of Allah in providing varying environments. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “There is no nation that does not have a remnant of guidance” (paraphrase). These people were likely uncivilized but still accountable to Allah according to their capacity. Dhul-Qarnain was a witness to this. The lesson: Believers must reflect on Allah’s creation and the different circumstances of people. Some lack even basic protection from the elements, yet Allah sustains them. It instills gratitude for one’s own comforts and a sense of responsibility to help the less fortunate. It also prepares for the next story of building a barrier for protection.
verse 91 | part 1 | So it was. And We truly had full knowledge of him. | The phrase “kadhālika” (So it was) closes the description of the eastern journey, affirming the truth of the account. “And We had full knowledge of what he had” (aḥaṭnā bimā ladayhi khubrā) means Allah’s knowledge encompassed all of Dhul-Qarnain’s resources, armies, power, and inner state. Nothing was hidden from Allah. This underscores that Dhul-Qarnain’s success was under divine supervision and with divine aid. The lesson: Allah is fully aware of the means and intentions of His servants. This is both a comfort for the righteous leader—that Allah knows his efforts and sincerity—and a warning that power and wealth are accounted for. The verse reinforces the theme that all earthly authority is granted by Allah, who is all-knowing, and the final judgment rests with Him. It also sets the stage for the continuation of the story in the following verses.
verse 92 | part 1 | Then he travelled a third course | After dealing with the east, Dhul-Qarnain set out on a third expedition, this time in a different direction, likely northward between the mountain ranges. The word “sababā” (course, means, path) appears again, emphasizing that he used the resources and knowledge Allah had given him. This third journey shows his relentless effort to establish justice and reach all corners of the earth. The Prophet (peace be upon him) was also a tireless traveler in spreading the message. His night journey and migration are examples. The lesson is that a righteous leader does not stop after one or two achievements; the mission to spread good, protect the weak, and establish order is continuous. Allah’s gifts of “means” must be fully utilized for noble purposes. This also teaches that the earth contains different terrains and peoples, and a believer may be called to remote places to serve humanity. The journey foreshadows the encounter with an oppressed people needing a barrier against Gog and Magog. It encourages believers to be proactive in solving global problems, using their God-given talents. Dhul-Qarnain’s example is of a king who doesn’t sit on a throne but goes to the frontiers himself. The verse highlights the virtue of personal involvement in public works.
verse 93 | part 1 | until he reached a pass between two mountains | Dhul-Qarnain arrived at a narrow passage between two mountains (bayna as-saddayn). This geographical feature was a choke point where a barrier could be effectively built. The two mountains acted as natural walls, leaving only a gap to be sealed. This shows divine wisdom in creation: the mountains were already placed as a partial defense. The Quran often mentions mountains as stabilizers and signs. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “When Allah created the earth, it started shaking, so He made the mountains firm on it” (Sunan Abu Dawud). Dhul-Qarnain’s military engineering genius lay in recognizing the strategic location. The two mountains symbolize the two forces between which a barrier is needed: civilization and chaos. The pass is a place of vulnerability that the righteous ruler must secure. Historically, many scholars identify this location as the Caucasus Mountains region. The lesson: a leader must first survey and understand the landscape before providing a solution. The mountains that Allah created serve multiple purposes, including protection for those who ask. Believers should reflect on natural defenses and use them, combining tawakkul (trust in Allah) with practical measures. It also teaches that strong foundations—like the two mountains—are needed to support the work of justice.
verse 93 | part 2 | He found in front of them a people who could hardly understand his language | Near the mountains, Dhul-Qarnain encountered a people whose language was foreign, almost unintelligible (lā yakādūna yafqahūna qawlā). This indicates a remote, isolated tribe with a different tongue. Despite the communication barrier, they managed to plead for help. This shows that the call for justice transcends language. The Prophet (peace be upon him) sent letters to various rulers in their own languages, demonstrating the importance of communication. The hadith narrates that Zayd ibn Thabit learned Hebrew and Syriac to correspond with Jews (Sunan Abu Dawud). The lesson: language barriers should not prevent delivering aid or establishing justice. The righteous ruler must find ways to understand the needs of all people under his domain, even if they speak different tongues. The story also reminds that Islam is a universal message; the Companions spread to non-Arab regions and learned local languages. Where communication is difficult, body language, signs, and interpreters can bridge gaps. Furthermore, the people’s plea indicates that even isolated communities know right from wrong and seek protection against oppressors. This is a fitrah (innate nature) that all people share. The duty of the strong is to listen, even when it’s hard to understand.
verse 94 | part 1 | They pleaded, “O Dhul-Qarnain! Surely Gog and Magog are spreading corruption throughout the land.” | The people described Ya’jūj and Ma’jūj (Gog and Magog) as corrupters spreading mischief (mufsidūn) in the land. Gog and Magog are mentioned in the Bible and in Islamic eschatology as wild, destructive tribes. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Gog and Magog are two nations; no one of them dies without leaving a thousand descendants” (part of a longer hadith in Sahih Muslim). They will emerge near the end of time as a major sign of the Hour. In this historical context, they were raiders causing devastation. The people turned to a just king for help, recognizing that only a powerful, righteous authority could stop such chaos. The plea “spreading corruption” encompasses murder, plunder, and destruction of crops. This aligns with the Islamic command to resist fasād (corruption) on earth. The early Muslims similarly faced corrupt Meccan oppressors. The lesson: when faced with overwhelming corruption, people should seek aid from those with capability and piety. The story underscores that security is a basic human need, and building defenses is a communal obligation. It also sets the stage for the barrier, showing that Allah can use a just ruler to contain evil for a time.
verse 94 | part 2 | Should we pay you tribute, provided that you build a wall between us and them? | The people offered kharj (tribute, tax, or payment) as compensation for building a protective wall (sadd). This shows they valued security above wealth and were willing to sacrifice. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The upper hand (the giver) is better than the lower hand (the receiver)” (Sahih Bukhari). They were ready to give their money for the common good. Dhul-Qarnain’s response later shows a higher ethic: refuse payment but accept physical help. Historically, Islamic rulers were allowed to impose taxes for public works like walls, bridges, and fortresses. This story illustrates the permissibility of funding defensive projects from public contributions. The word “sadd” refers to a massive barrier, not a small wall, indicating an engineering marvel. The people’s request was reasonable, and they did not ask for fighting but for a barrier, preferring deterrence. Lesson: communities should invest in their collective security, even bearing financial burdens. The willingness to pay for protection reveals the severity of Gog and Magog’s threat. In modern contexts, this can apply to funding defense and public infrastructure. It also demonstrates that they respected Dhul-Qarnain as a trustworthy, capable leader, a trust he honored by declining their tribute.
verse 95 | part 1 | He responded, “What my Lord has provided for me is far better.” | Dhul-Qarnain refused personal financial gain, stating that Allah’s provisions—wealth, power, and resources—were far superior (khayr) to any tribute they could offer. This reflects the nobility of a leader who does not exploit his position for enrichment. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “We, the prophets, do not leave behind inheritance; whatever we leave is for charity” (Sahih Bukhari). He also forbade taking gifts in official capacity. Dhul-Qarnain’s attitude was similar: he saw his kingdom as a trust from Allah, so he did not need to charge the oppressed. This is a powerful lesson for all those in authority: public service is an amānah (trust), and one should not demand worldly compensation from the weak. The phrase “my Lord” shows his humility; he attributed all his wealth to Allah. This prevented arrogance. He used what Allah gave him to serve humanity for free. The lesson: those blessed with abundance should use it in Allah’s cause without expecting material reward from people. It’s the ultimate act of gratitude. The statement also reinforces that Allah’s reward is far better than any worldly payment.
verse 95 | part 2 | But assist me with resources, and I will build a barrier between you and them. | Instead of money, Dhul-Qarnain asked for quwwah (physical strength, manpower, resources) to help in the construction. He would provide the expertise and leadership; they would provide the labor and materials. This fosters cooperation and dignity: they are not passive recipients but active partners. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The believer to another believer is like a building, each part strengthening the other” (Sahih Bukhari). The word “radman” (barrier) suggests a massive embankment or dam, more than a simple wall. The collaboration between ruler and people is an Islamic principle: shūrā (consultation) and collective effort. The people’s strength was their numbers and knowledge of the terrain. The lesson: the best projects are those where the leader and the community work hand in hand, each contributing what they can. It preserves self-respect and ensures sustainability. The barrier would not be a handout but a joint achievement. In modern society, this is like public-private partnership for public goods. It also teaches that while material help might be refused, human effort and solidarity are invaluable.
verse 96 | part 1 | Bring me blocks of iron! Then, when he had filled up the gap between the two mountains, he ordered, “Blow!” | Dhul-Qarnain launched the engineering project: he ordered iron blocks (zubar al-ḥadīd) to be brought and stacked to fill the gap between the mountains. When the gap was leveled with iron, he commanded them to blow (unfukhū) with bellows to create intense heat, turning the iron into a red-hot molten mass. This is a sophisticated metallurgical process. The Prophet (peace be upon him) described that the barrier was made of iron and copper, and that it is smooth like a sheet (from hadith about Gog and Magog digging at it daily). This technique—layering iron and then fusing with copper—created an almost impenetrable wall. The command “Blow!” indicates the use of large furnaces and sustained effort. The hadith says that Gog and Magog try to breach it each day but are unable. The lesson: strong defense requires knowledge, technology, and collective effort. Iron symbolizes strength, and Allah mentions iron in the Quran as having mighty power (57:25). This teaches Muslims to master science and industry for the protection of the ummah. The barrier was a physical manifestation of the divine aid that comes when people combine faith with practical skills.
verse 96 | part 2 | When the iron became red hot, he said, “Bring me molten copper to pour over it.” | After the iron was heated to a molten state, he ordered molten copper (qiṭr) to be poured over it, fusing the blocks into a solid, unified wall. Copper is resistant to corrosion and would fill the gaps, creating a seamless surface. This detailed description is a miracle of the Quran, as it outlines a two-material composite barrier. Modern engineering uses similar principles of reinforcing iron with other metals. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Gog and Magog dig into it every day, but when they are about to break through, they return to find it restored as before” (Sahih Bukhari). This barrier was so strong that it could only be breached by the will of Allah at the appointed time. The pouring of molten copper symbolizes the completion of the project and the merging of resources. Lesson: thorough work and using the best available materials are part of Islamic work ethic. Sloppiness is not from faith. The barrier’s perfection shows that when believers undertake a task, they should do it with excellence (iḥsān). The cooperation between the powerful ruler’s technology and the local manpower resulted in a lasting defense, a metaphor for how the ummah should unite to face common threats.
verse 97 | part 1 | And so the enemies could neither scale nor tunnel through it. | The completed barrier was too high to climb over (lam yasṭā‘ū an yaẓharūhu) and too strong and deep to penetrate or tunnel through (lam yastaṭī‘ū lahu naqbā). Gog and Magog were fully contained. This gave security to the people. The word “naqb” means a hole or breach; they could not bore through it. This is a testament to the engineering success. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The barrier of Gog and Magog has a gate, and they will break through it when the time comes” (paraphrase, but authentic hadiths state they dig daily but fail until Allah wills). The absolute security provided was temporary, as nothing permanent except Paradise. However, it served its purpose for a long era. The lesson: with divine guidance, human ingenuity can create effective protections against even the most savage threats. It also highlights the importance of securing borders and protecting the innocent. The barrier’s invincibility, while it lasted, was a mercy from Allah, showing that He can restrain evil through righteous leadership. It also prefigures the final release of Gog and Magog before the Day of Judgment, reminding that ultimate control is with Allah.
verse 98 | part 1 | He declared, “This is a mercy from my Lord.” | Upon completing the barrier, Dhul-Qarnain immediately attributed the success to Allah’s mercy (raḥmah), not his own skill. This is the hallmark of a true believer: shunning self-praise. The Prophet (peace be upon him) would say, “Whatever good reaches you, it is from Allah” (Sahih Bukhari). The barrier was a protection for the people, a sheer mercy. By calling it “mercy from my Lord,” he acknowledged that the iron, copper, and manpower were all provided by Allah, and the success was by His will. This humility protected him from the corruption of power. The lesson: after any achievement, a believer says “This is from my Lord’s mercy,” recognizing that all blessings flow from Allah. Gratitude increases blessings. Leaders must deflect praise to Allah to stay grounded. The phrase also instilled in the people that the barrier is not a deity or an object of worship; it’s a temporary gift. This safeguards against shirk. The early Muslims, after victories, would prostrate in thanks to Allah, following the prophetic model.
verse 98 | part 2 | But when the promise of my Lord comes to pass, He will level it to the ground. And my Lord’s promise is ever true. | Dhul-Qarnain prophesied that the barrier, no matter how strong, will be destroyed when Allah’s promise of the Hour arrives. The word “wa‘d rabbī” refers to the appointed time for Gog and Magog’s release. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Gog and Magog will break through the barrier and come down to attack the people, and they will drink up the waters…” (Sahih Muslim). Then Jesus will pray against them, and they will be destroyed. The barrier being leveled (ja‘alahu dakkā’) indicates complete flattening. This recognition of impermanence is crucial: even the greatest human achievements are temporary. The phrase “my Lord’s promise is ever true” affirms absolute certainty in the afterlife and divine decree. Lesson: believers should not place ultimate trust in material defenses, but in Allah. All worldly safeguards are temporary; only Allah’s word endures. This teaches detachment and preparedness for the inevitable end. It also comforts the oppressed that oppressors and their walls will fall. The early Muslims, facing mighty empires, trusted that Allah’s promise of victory was true.
verse 99 | part 1 | On that Day, We will let them surge like waves over one another. | On the day the barrier is destroyed, Gog and Magog will rush out in massive, chaotic hordes, like waves crashing into each other (mawj fī ba‘ḍ). This imagery conveys overwhelming numbers and unstoppable momentum. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Then Gog and Magog will come out, and they will spread corruption in the land. The first of them will pass by the Lake of Tiberias and drink from it, and the last of them will say, ‘There was once water here’” (Sahih Muslim). This surging signifies the final major tribulation before the end. The lesson: Allah can unleash stored forces at His will. No human barrier—physical or political—can permanently stop what Allah has decreed. This reminds believers to always be conscious of the approaching Hour and to remain steadfast in faith during cataclysms. It also shows that evil may be contained for a time, but it will eventually be released as a test.
verse 99 | part 2 | Later, the Trumpet will be blown, and We will gather all people together. | After the chaos of Gog and Magog and their subsequent destruction, the Trumpet (Ṣūr) will be blown by the angel Isrāfīl, signaling the end of the world and the resurrection. All of humanity, from first to last, will be gathered for judgment. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “How can I relax when the angel of the Trumpet has put his lips to the Trumpet and is waiting for the command?” (Sunan At-Tirmidhi). The gathering (jam‘ā) is complete, leaving none behind. This verse transitions from the historical story of Dhul-Qarnain to the universal eschatological reality. The lesson: the stories of past nations and the warnings of future tribulations all point to the inescapable Day of Gathering. Believers must live each day as if preparing for that trumpet blast, when all worldly distinctions will vanish and only one’s deeds will matter. It connects the temporary barrier to the ultimate justice of Allah.
verse 100 | part 1 | On that Day We will display Hell clearly for the disbelievers | On the Day of Judgment, Hell (Jahannam) will be brought forth and visibly presented to the disbelievers. The word “‘arḍnā” suggests a prominent display, so that they see its terror and realize their fate. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Hellfire will be brought on that Day, dragged by seventy thousand ropes, each rope pulled by seventy thousand angels” (Sahih Muslim). This visible presentation is part of their humiliation and punishment. The disbelievers used to deny Hell’s existence; now it is undeniable. The specific mention of “disbelievers” indicates that the display is a prelude to their entry, a moment of extreme dread. The lesson: the unseen realities that people mock will become manifest. The visualization of Hell serves as a deterrent for the living: imagine that Day when the Fire is right before your eyes. It is a call to immediate faith and repentance, so that one is not among those for whom Hell is displayed as a destination. This scene is the ultimate justice for those who rejected truth.
verse 101 | part 1 | those who turned a blind eye to My Reminder | The disbelievers are characterized as those whose eyes were covered (kānat a‘yunuhum fī ghiṭā’) from Allah’s dhikr (Reminder)—the Quran and divine signs. They deliberately ignored the truth. The phrase “blind eye” indicates not physical blindness but willful neglect. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The one who hears the call to Islam and does not respond, his face will be darkened on the Day of Resurrection” (paraphrase). The eyes are the gates of insight; when they are covered, the heart becomes blind. The Quraysh saw the Prophet’s miracles but denied them out of arrogance. This covering was a consequence of their own sins, as Allah says elsewhere: “It is not the eyes that are blind, but the hearts” (22:46). Lesson: spiritual blindness is worse than physical blindness. One must actively remove the veils of desire and prejudice to see the truth. The Reminder—the Quran—is clear, but its light only benefits those who open their eyes. The warning is against taking revelation for granted.
verse 101 | part 2 | and could not stand listening to it | Not only were they blind, but they were unable to hear (kānū lā yastaṭī‘ūna sam‘ā) the Quran—meaning they had no capacity or willingness to listen sincerely. Their hearts were so hardened that they could not bear to hear the truth. The Quraysh used to put fingers in their ears and make noise to avoid hearing the Prophet (41:26). The inability here stems from their own rejection, which Allah then sealed. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “If you ask Allah for Paradise, ask Him for al-Firdaws, which is the middle of Paradise and the highest part… and from it gush the rivers of Paradise” (Sahih Bukhari). Those who cannot listen lose this. Lesson: the faculty of hearing guidance is a gift that can be lost through persistent sin. One must guard their ears and heart, ensuring they are receptive to the truth. The verse shows that ultimate loss is the inability to even stand listening to what can save them. It is a warning against mockery and distraction, and a call to give full, humble attention to Allah’s words.
verse 102 | part 1 | Do the disbelievers think they can simply take My servants as lords instead of Me? | This rebukes those who worship prophets, angels, saints, or idols as lords besides Allah. “My servants” refers to Jesus, Ezra, the angels, or any righteous beings whom people deify. The disbelievers think they can take them as awliyā’ (protectors, lords) without consequence. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Do not exaggerate in praising me as the Christians exaggerated in praising the son of Mary. I am only a servant, so say: the servant of Allah and His Messenger” (Sahih Bukhari). The verse exposes the folly of thinking that such worship will be accepted or beneficial. The phrase “a-ḥasiba” (do they think) implies a miscalculation. Historically, the Christians took Jesus as son of God, and the pagans took idols as intercessors. Allah is the only Lord; taking others is the ultimate injustice. The lesson: shirk is a grave delusion. No servant of Allah, no matter how righteous, deserves worship. The verse calls to pure monotheism and warns that those taken as lords will disown their worshippers on Judgment Day. It safeguards the exclusive worship of Allah.
verse 102 | part 2 | We have surely prepared Hell as an accommodation for the disbelievers. | The consequence for such shirk is Hell, described as nuzul (accommodation, hospitality) in an ironic, terrifying way. Nuzul originally means the food prepared for a guest. Here, the “hospitality” is eternal punishment. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “When the month of Ramadan starts, the gates of Paradise are opened, the gates of Hell are closed, and the devils are chained” (Sahih Bukhari). But for the disbelievers, Hell is always prepared. The word “a‘tadnā” indicates it is already prepared, waiting. This emphasizes the certainty and nearness of the punishment. The lesson: disbelief and shirk are not trivial matters; they warrant the worst accommodation. The imagery of “hospitality” contrasts with the “gardens of Paradise” as accommodation for believers (verse 107), creating a stark choice. It is a stern warning to avoid associating partners with Allah and to adhere to tawḥīd.
verse 103 | part 1 | Say, O Prophet, “Shall we inform you of who will lose the most deeds?” | The Prophet is commanded to ask a rhetorical question to draw attention. “Al-akhsarīn a‘mālā” means those in the greatest loss regarding their deeds. The verse stirs curiosity: who are the biggest losers? The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The bankrupt of my ummah is the one who comes on the Day of Judgment with prayer, fasting, and charity, but he insulted this one, slandered that one, devoured the wealth of this one…” (Sahih Muslim). This hadith shows loss of deeds due to wrongs against people, but here the focus is on those whose deeds are void from the start. The question style makes the listener reflect. Historically, the Meccans thought their charitable deeds and rituals would benefit them despite shirk. Allah reveals the shocking truth. The lesson: not all effort is rewarded; it must meet the criteria of faith and sincerity. Believers should fear the loss of deeds and ensure their actions are for Allah alone, according to the Sunnah. It’s a warning against self-deception.
verse 104 | part 1 | They are those whose efforts are in vain in this worldly life, while they think they are doing good! | These losers are those who strive hard in the world (sa‘yuhum) but their efforts are misguided (ḍalla sa‘yuhum). They perform actions they perceive as good (yaḥsabūna annahum yuḥsinūna ṣun‘ā), yet they will be rejected. This applies to disbelievers, polytheists, and innovators whose deeds lack faith or correct intention. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Actions are by intentions, and every person will get what he intended” (Sahih Bukhari). Someone may build hospitals, feed the poor, or practice rigorous devotion, but if it’s for other than Allah or not based on revelation, it’s in vain. The Quraysh fed pilgrims and maintained the Kaaba, but their shirk nullified it. The lesson: sincerity and conformity to the Prophet’s way are essential. The scariest part is “they think they are doing good”; self-deception is easy. Believers must regularly check their hearts and knowledge. Good intentions without correct guidance lead to ruin. This verse is a call to learn the Quran and Sunnah, so actions are not wasted.
verse 105 | part 1 | It is they who reject the signs of their Lord and their meeting with Him, rendering their deeds void | The reason their deeds are vain: they reject (kafarū) the āyāt (signs) of Allah and deny the meeting with Him (liqā’ihi). Rejection of revelation and the Hereafter corrupts the foundation, so deeds collapse. Good deeds must be built on īmān. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Whoever rejects faith, his works are in vain, and he, in the Hereafter, will be among the losers” (5:5). The voiding (ḥabiṭat) means they carry no weight, like scattered dust. Historical context: the disbelievers of Makkah did many noble acts by Arab standards but rejected tawḥīd and resurrection. Their bravery, generosity, and pilgrim services were worthless. Lesson: correct creed is the essence. Without it, even the greatest worldly achievements evaporate. It is a warning to prioritize faith over all else. The verse encourages dawah to the fundamentals: belief in Allah, His signs, and the Last Day. For the believer, it reinforces that deeds are only safe if they are connected to the hope of meeting Allah.
verse 105 | part 2 | so We will not give their deeds any weight on Judgment Day | Allah will not assign any weight (wazn) or value to their deeds on the Scales. Even if the deeds were many, they are treated as nothing. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Two kalimahs are light on the tongue, heavy on the Scale, beloved to the Most Merciful: Subḥān Allāh wa biḥamdihī, Subḥān Allāh al-‘Aẓīm” (Sahih Bukhari). On the contrary, the disbelievers’ deeds are weightless because they lack īmān. The setting up of the Scales is a reality; deeds are weighed. The phrase “nūzinu lahum” implies a scale with no good in it for them. The lesson: only deeds founded on faith and sincerity have eternal weight. The Scales of the Day of Judgment are sensitive to the quality, not quantity. It warns that a person may be surprised to find their lifelong work amounting to zero. Believers should focus on secret good deeds, pure faith, and following the Sunnah, so their scale is heavy. It’s a motivator to make every action count for the Hereafter.
verse 106 | part 1 | That is their reward: Hell | The wage (jazā’) for such people is Jahannam. The word “dhālikum” emphasizes that what was described—weightless deeds—results in this specific punishment. The reward matches the crime: they belittled Allah’s signs, so they are placed in the most abased state. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The least punished person in Hell will have his brain boiling from a fire under his feet” (Sahih Bukhari). This is the inevitable outcome of rejecting faith. Lesson: the severity of the punishment reflects the severity of the crime of disbelief and mockery. It should instill a healthy fear that drives one away from anything that nullifies faith. The mention of “reward” (jazā’) is ironic: they earned this through their choices. Believers must thank Allah for faith and never take it for granted.
verse 106 | part 2 | for their disbelief and mockery of My signs and messengers. | Two specific reasons for Hell: kufr (disbelief) and sikhriyya (mockery) of Allah’s signs and messengers. They not only disbelieved but actively ridiculed the Quran, the Prophet, and the warnings. The Quraysh called the Prophet a madman, magician, and poet. They laughed at the verses. The Prophet (peace be upon him) was deeply hurt by their mockery. This verse confirms that mockery is a major sin that leads to Hell. Allah says elsewhere, “If you ask them, they will say, ‘We were only conversing and playing.’ Say, ‘Is it Allah and His signs and His messenger that you were mocking?’” (9:65). Lesson: respect for revelation and messengers is obligatory. Sarcasm and ridicule of religion are characteristics of hypocrites and disbelievers. Believers must honor the sacred symbols of Allah and avoid even joking about them. The punishment for mockery coupled with disbelief is eternal, highlighting the gravity of belittling divine truth.
verse 107 | part 1 | Indeed, those who believe and do good will have the Gardens of Paradise as an accommodation | After the warning comes the glad tiding. Believers who combine īmān and ṣāliḥāt are promised Jannāt al-Firdaws. “Firdaws” is the highest, most central level of Paradise. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “When you ask Allah, ask Him for al-Firdaws, for it is the middle of Paradise and the highest part of Paradise” (Sahih Bukhari). The word “nuzul” (accommodation) again appears, contrasting with Hell as the accommodation for disbelievers. The hospitality here is eternal bliss. This promise gave hope to the early Muslims who suffered persecution and poverty. They would be hosted in unimaginable luxury. Lesson: true success is in Paradise, not in worldly recognition. The combination of internal belief and external good deeds is the price. The believer’s present hardship is a temporary journey toward the ultimate hospitality. It encourages consistency in good works, knowing that Allah’s hospitality awaits.
verse 108 | part 1 | where they will be forever, never desiring anywhere else | The believers will abide eternally (khālidīna) in Paradise, never wishing to move or be transferred (lā yabghūna ‘anhā ḥiwalā). This indicates complete satisfaction; no boredom, no desire for change. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “The one who enters Paradise will enjoy bliss and never be miserable; his clothes will never wear out, and his youth will never fade” (Sahih Muslim). The “ḥiwal” means shifting to another place; they will never request such a thing. This is the ultimate peace of mind. The earlier description of Dhul-Qarnain’s temporary barrier contrasts with this eternal, unbreachable abode. Lesson: the eternal nature of Paradise makes every worldly sacrifice worthwhile. The believer’s heart should long for this permanence, not the fleeting world. The verse also assures that in Paradise there is no fear of eviction or change—absolute security.
verse 109 | part 1 | Say, O Prophet, “If the ocean were ink for writing the Words of my Lord, it would certainly run out | This magnificent verse illustrates the infiniteness of Allah’s knowledge and words. If all the oceans became ink (midād) to write the kalimāt (words) of Allah—meaning His knowledge, decrees, creations, and revelations—the ocean would be exhausted before the words were finished. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “If all the trees on earth were pens and the ocean (ink) were supplied by seven more oceans, the words of Allah would not be exhausted” (paraphrase of Sahih Muslim). The Quraysh sometimes questioned the scope of revelation; this verse shows that what has been revealed is a drop from an infinite ocean. Lesson: human beings can never fully comprehend the vastness of divine knowledge. This cultivates humility and wonder. It also refutes any claim that the Quran is the final limit of Allah’s knowledge; rather, it is what He has chosen to reveal from His infinite words. Believers should appreciate the honor of having access to these words, while acknowledging the overwhelming majesty of the Speaker.
verse 109 | part 2 | before the Words of my Lord were finished, even if We refilled it with its equal | The ocean and its replenishment would still fall short. Even if another ocean equal in size (bimithlihi madadā) were brought, it would not suffice. This emphasizes mathematical infinity. No finite amount can capture the infinite. Allah’s words encompass everything He has said, will say, and could say; they are inexhaustible. The use of “We refilled” indicates divine power. The lesson: the knowledge and creative capacity of Allah are beyond all comparison. This should make the believer feel awe and also immense trust: the One whose words are infinite can surely solve any problem. It also warns against those who think they have fully grasped divine wisdom; the ocean of wisdom is endless, and the seeker of knowledge should remain humble. The early Sufis saw this as a call to perpetual learning and dhikr.
verse 110 | part 1 | Say, O Prophet, “I am only a man like you, but it has been revealed to me that your God is only One God.” | The final verse emphasizes the humanity and messengership of Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). He is a human (bashar), not an angel or divine being, but distinguished by waḥy (revelation). The core of that revelation is pure monotheism: “Your God is one God.” This echoes the start of the surah and the entire Quranic message. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Do not exaggerate in praising me as the Christians did with Jesus. I am only a servant. So say: the servant of Allah and His Messenger” (Sahih Bukhari). The Meccans had expected a supernatural king; the verse corrects that. It also elevates humanity: a man can be the recipient of divine speech. The lesson: Islam is free from the deification of messengers. The Prophet is a model for humans; his teachings are attainable. The monotheism declared here is the essence of faith. Believers are called to follow a fellow human who is guided, making the religion accessible and relatable.
verse 110 | part 2 | So whoever hopes for the meeting with their Lord, let them do good deeds and associate none in the worship of their Lord. | The verse ends with the condition for a successful meeting with Allah: perform righteous deeds (amalan ṣāliḥan) and absolutely avoid shirk (lā yushrik bi‘ibādati rabbihi aḥadā). This combines orthopraxy and orthodoxy. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, “Allah will say: ‘I am in no need of partners. Whoever does a deed for Me and associates someone else with Me, I will reject him and his association’” (Sahih Muslim). The hope (yarjū) in meeting Allah must be validated by pure worship. The deed must be in accordance with the Sunnah and for Allah alone. Historical context: this was a direct message to those who worshipped idols, angels, or prophets, that even the Prophet Muhammad himself worshipped only Allah. The lesson is the ultimate summary of the Quran: sincere monotheism and righteous action lead to eternal bliss. Every believer should assess their deeds: Are they solely for Allah? Are they as the Prophet taught? This verse is a constant check and the path to the Garden described earlier. It perfectly concludes Surah Al-Kahf, providing the practical takeaway from all its stories.